window.__registerChapter({"num": 1, "slug": "001_Finding_the_Rare_Among_the_Common_Diagnosis_Diagnostic_Error_and_Whe", "title": "Finding the Rare Among the Common: Diagnosis, Diagnostic Error, and When to Suspect a Rare Disease", "authors": "Brett J. Bordini, Donald G. Basel", "part_num": "I", "part_title": "Introductory Chapters", "word_count": 5637, "pearls": ["This dual process theory suggests that most clinicians, under most circumstances, employ a system 1 process with relatively high diagnostic accuracy for most common medical concerns.", "Certain rare disease phenotypes are well characterized and are rapidly recognized when presenting with typical manifestations; others are readily diagnosed based on newborn screening results or an established family history.", "Some rare disorders do present with pathognomonic historical, physical, laboratory, or imaging findings that allow for rapid diagnosis.", "The field of rare disorders is relatively nascent, having only formally organized and evolved over the last several decades, yet literary descriptions of rare disorders can be found across the globe dating back thousands of years.", "Fundamental to the practice of medicine is diagnosis, the process of uncovering the cause of a patient’s health-related concerns.", "Rare disorders face unique barriers to timely and accurate diagnosis, including limited clinician recognition and knowledge.", "Medical education is structured to provide clinicians with the ability to readily recognize the key historical and physical features of common disorders and to seek common explanations for patient concerns."], "genes": [], "omims": [], "subtopics": ["Introductory Chapters", "Finding the Rare Among the Common Diagnosis, Diagnostic Error, and When to Suspect a Rare Disease"], "figs": [{"img": "fig_p0029_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 1.1", "caption": "The diagnostic process as it occurs within the larger sociotechnical healthcare work system. The patient and diagnosing clinician exist as part of a larger system containing other members of the diagnostic team, all interacting with various technologies and tools in a dynamically changing environment working toward completion of the common task of diagnosis. Changes in any of these variables can positively or negatively impact the outcome. (Balogh, E.P., Miller, B.T., Ball, J.R., 2015. Improving Diagnosis in Health Care . National Academies Press, Washington, DC.)"}, {"img": "fig_p0029_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 1.1", "caption": "The diagnostic process as it occurs within the larger sociotechnical healthcare work system. The patient and diagnosing clinician exist as part of a larger system containing other members of the diagnostic team, all interacting with various technologies and tools in a dynamically changing environment working toward completion of the common task of diagnosis. Changes in any of these variables can positively or negatively impact the outcome. (Balogh, E.P., Miller, B.T., Ball, J.R., 2015. Improving Diagnosis in Health Care . National Academies Press, Washington, DC.)"}, {"img": "vfig_p0029_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 1.2", "caption": "The diagnostic odyssey, from first appearance of symptoms to confirmed diagnosis. (Adapted from Soomers V, Husson O, Young R, Desar I, Van der Graaf W. The sarcoma diagnostic interval: a systematic review on length, contributing factors and patient outcomes. ESMO Open . 2020 Feb;5[1]:e000592."}, {"img": "fig_p0031_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 1.2", "caption": "The diagnostic odyssey, from first appearance of symptoms to confirmed diagnosis. (Adapted from Soomers V, Husson O, Young R, Desar I, Van der Graaf W. The sarcoma diagnostic interval: a systematic review on length, contributing factors and patient outcomes. ESMO Open . 2020 Feb;5[1]:e000592."}], "tables_meta": [{"label": "TABLE 1.1", "title": "Cognitive Bias es Related to Heuristic Failure"}, {"label": "TABLE 1.2", "title": "Cognitive Bia ses Related to Errors of Attribution"}, {"label": "Table 1.3", "title": "Bias Definition"}], "body_md": "\n<!-- p.22 -->\n\n## SECTION **I** \n\n#### Introductory Chapters \n\n1. Finding the Rare Among the Common: Diagnosis, Diagnostic Error, and When to Suspect a Rare Disease, 3 \n\n2. Diagnostic Methods: Genomics, Omics, Biomarkers, Imaging, and Other Technologies, 13 \n\n3. The Approach to Rare Diseases: From Local to Global, 41\n\n<!-- p.23 -->\n\n\n\n<!-- p.24 -->\n\n# 1 \n\n### Finding the Rare Among the Common Diagnosis, Diagnostic Error, and When to Suspect a Rare Disease \n\n**BRETT J. BORDINI, DONALD G. BASEL** \n\nThe field of rare disorders is relatively nascent, having only formally organized and evolved over the last several decades, yet literary descriptions of rare disorders can be found across the globe dating back thousands of years. Over the last 100 to 200 years, advances in microbiology and environmental sciences improved the management of many acquired rare conditions, while only more recently have developments in genomic sciences and precision medicine led to better recognition, diagnosis, and treatment of rare heritable disorders. Much of this advancement in the care of rare heritable disorders has historically relied on the networking, organization, and advocacy of patients and families, as well as researchers and clinicians with interests in rare disorders. This grassroots approach has proven fruitful, leading to the development of broad national and international consortia dedicated to undiagnosed and rare diseases. Through the rare disease community, many unmet needs in diagnosis, disease-specific therapies, and broader societal support have been identified. \n\nThere are more individuals with rare disorders across the globe than there are with many individual common disorders. Definitions of rarity vary throughout the world (see Chapter 3); in the United States, a disorder is considered rare if it affects fewer than 200,000 individuals. Collectively, more than 7,000 identified rare disorders affect nearly 6% of the population or up to 450 million individuals globally, with significant associated physical, emotional, social, and financial burdens. Up to 80% of rare diseases have or are presumed to have a genetic etiology; approximately half affect the pediatric population. These factors of heritability and oftentimes early onset compound to create significant and prolonged stressors across the lifespan. The economic impact of rare diseases is substantial, with annual direct and indirect medical expenditures estimated at approximately $8 trillion (USD) globally. The cost of rare disease diagnosis and care far exceed those for many common disorders; \n\nattaining a diagnosis and implementing specific treatment plans can lower these costs significantly. Despite these demonstrable costs and benefits, rare disorders have historically had far fewer resources allocated to their diagnosis and management. Over 95% of rare diseases lack specific disease-directed therapies; research for rare disorders has often required incentivization. \n\n_Diagnosis is essential to improving individual and broader societal outcomes for people affected by rare disorders._ Providing an accurate diagnosis permits rational treatment plans to be developed even in the absence of targeted therapies, as non-beneficial aspects of management can be eliminated, thus reducing the risk of iatrogenic harm and minimizing the amount of time invested in daily cares. Diagnosis informs reproductive counseling and prognostication, enrollment in clinical or research protocols, and connection with other individuals affected by the same or related disorders. Diagnosis can be challenging for rare and common disorders alike; there are unique challenges in diagnosing rare disorders. While some have pathognomonic presentations that allow for rapid diagnosis, most rare disorders—by virtue of infrequency, heterogeneity, subtlety, capacity to mimic more common disorders, or lack of established methods for diagnosis—are inherently more prone to _diagnostic error_ and are more likely to be affected by limitations in _diagnostic access_ . \n\n##### **Diagnosis and Diagnostic Error** \n\nFundamental to the practice of medicine is diagnosis, the process of uncovering the cause of a patient’s health-related concerns. The diagnostic process is _iterative_ , typically proceeding stepwise from an undifferentiated symptom or concern to an established cause and appropriately tailored plan of care. Arriving at a diagnosis first begins with establishing the **history** of the patient’s primary concern and\n\n<!-- p.25 -->\n\nplacing that concern in the context of the patient’s personal, family, social, and environmental histories to determine what internal or exogenous factors may be contributing. **Physical examination** then provides important phenotypic information; both the presence and absence of key findings inform the list of diagnoses under consideration (i.e., the differential diagnosis) and guide diagnostic testing strategies. The interpretation of the history, physical examination, and diagnostic testing results is best conceptualized not as a process of dichotomously ruling in or ruling out certain conditions, but rather as a method of determining the relative significance of each component in establishing the likelihood of a particular diagnosis. While this iterative approach to diagnosis is meant to ensure consistency and comprehensiveness, diagnosis is also _dynamic_ , wherein sudden changes in clinical status or the uncovering of new historical information may significantly alter the diagnostic formulation or the urgency of the evaluation. \n\nDiagnosis is also _collaborative_ , valuing input from not only the patient and primary clinician, but also from family, caregivers, community partners such as teachers who know the patient well, and all members of the healthcare team. This **team-based approach** to diagnosis incorporates diverse points of view and expands the collective knowledge base, often outperforming the diagnostic accuracy of individual experienced diagnosticians. Even when diagnostic evaluations are occurring asynchronously and in fragmented specialty care settings, open communication and close collaboration among disciplines minimize delays and redundancies in testing. \n\nDespite these historically well-established approaches to diagnosis, the process has the potential for error, in both rare and common disorders alike. **Diagnostic error** is _the failure to (a) establish an accurate and timely explanation of the patient’s health problem(s) or (b) communicate that explanation to the patient._ Diagnostic error can be defined from a variety of perspectives. The _type_ of error, for example, can consist of a missed, delayed, or wrong diagnosis. Many patients with rare disorders experience diagnostic error, especially from diagnostic delays. The _root causes_ of diagnostic error are diverse; they can be broadly categorized as _no-fault_ , wherein disorders present with atypical features or diagnosis is hindered secondary to a patient being unwilling or otherwise unable to participate in the evaluation; _systemsrelated_ , wherein organizational or technical factors such as the unavailability of a particular subspecialty service or testing modality obscure diagnosis; or _cognitive_ , wherein faulty clinician knowledge, data gathering, or medical reasoning produces errors. The majority of diagnostic errors are solely or at least partially attributable to cognitive errors on the part of diagnosticians; most cognitive errors are related to the ways in which various forms of cognitive bias can be introduced into diagnostic reasoning. \n\nThe individual diagnosing clinician, when presented with patient concerns, typically engages one of two cognitive processing systems to assess available information and arrive at a diagnosis, either a **system 1 process** that is rapid, \n\nintuitive, and based in pattern recognition and rules of thumb termed _heuristics_ , or a more deliberate and analytical **system 2 process** that is based in hypothesis generation and counterfactual reasoning. This **dual process theory** suggests that most clinicians, under most circumstances, employ a system 1 process with relatively high diagnostic accuracy for most common medical concerns. However, these same system 1 processes are prone to fail when patient presentations are complex, multisystem, slowly evolving, or undifferentiated, instead becoming a form of **cognitive bias** that can lead to diagnostic error. \n\nCognitive biases impacting medical reasoning can be classified as _heuristic failure_ , _errors of attribution_ , or _contextrelated biases._ With **heuristic failure** , the same rules of thumb and quick pattern recognition that clinicians employ to arrive at a diagnosis fail to account for the entire scope of patient information or fail to adapt to new information as it becomes available and instead bias the clarity of medical reasoning. While heuristics can fail in a variety of ways (Table 1.1), several are particularly relevant to individuals with rare diseases. **Anchoring** occurs when a clinician locks into a diagnosis based on the initial presenting features, failing to adjust diagnostic impressions when new clinical information presents itself. **Confirmation bias** occurs when clinicians seek out or respond only to evidence that affirms diagnostic impressions, to the neglect of contradictory evidence. **Diagnostic momentum** , wherein diagnostic labels are copied forward over time without being questioned, despite those labels oftentimes being outdated, incomplete, or even inaccurate, can be a significant source of cognitive bias for patients with rare disorders, especially when electronic medical records allow the perpetuation of diagnostic labels to be a more passive process. \n\nCognitive bias related to **errors of attribution** occurs when perceived characteristics of patients, family members, or members of the medical evaluation team are given undue weight in the diagnostic formulation, or when the emotional state of the clinician influences cognition (Table 1.2). Important examples in the evaluation of rare disorders include the **appeal to authority** , wherein senior, supervising, or otherwise expert recommendations are treated as authoritative, independent of whether evidence supports those recommendations. Statements such as “I’ve never seen this disease present in that way,” when coming from a senior, experienced clinician, can be given undue emphasis and remove certain diagnoses from consideration. **Normalcy biases** may occur when clinicians de-emphasize the importance of certain results by labeling them as “slightly” or “mildly” abnormal, rather than treating them as objective data points to be interpreted within the context of the patient’s physiology and pathophysiology, particularly if influenced by a conscious or subconscious desire for a patient to have a benign explanation for abnormal findings. Patients with a history of mental health concerns, or with symptoms that do not immediately suggest organic pathology, can be labeled as having symptoms secondary to a functional diagnosis when organic pathology may be the sole cause.\n\n<!-- p.26 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 1.1** Cognitive Bias es Related to Heuristic Failure\n\n|Bias|Definition|\n|---|---|\n|Anchoring|Locking into a diagnosis based on initial presenting features, failing to adjust diagnostic impressions<br>when new information becomes available.|\n|Confirmation bias|Looking for and accepting only evidence that confirms a diagnostic impression, rejecting or not seeking<br>contradictory evidence.|\n|Diagnostic momentum|Perpetuating a diagnostic label over time, usually by multiple providers both within and across health<br>care systems, despite the label being incomplete or inaccurate.|\n|Premature closure|Accepting the first plausible diagnosis prior to obtaining confirmatory evidence or considering all<br>available evidence.|\n|Unpacking principle|Failing to explore primary evidence or data in its entirety and subsequently failing to uncover important<br>facts or findings, such as accepting a biopsy report or imaging study report without reviewing the<br>actual specimen or image.|\n|From Bordini BJ, Stephany<br>jpeds.2017.02.065.|A, Kliegman R. Overcoming Diagnostic Errors in Medical Practice._J Pediatr._2017;185:19-25.e1.https://doi.org/10.1016/j.|\n\n\n\n#### **TABLE 1.2** Cognitive Bia ses Related to Errors of Attribution\n\n|Bias|Definition|\n|---|---|\n|Appeal to authority|Deferring to authoritative recommendations from senior, supervising, or “expert” clinicians, independent of<br>the evidentiary support for such recommendations.|\n|Countertransference|Being influenced by positive or negative subjective feelings toward a specific patient.|\n|Normalcy bias|Minimizing or overemphasizing the significance of a finding or result, often based on subjective feelings<br>about a patient or a desired outcome; the use of “slightly” to describe abnormal results.|\n|Mental health bias|Maintaining biases about people with presumed mental health concerns; assuming that symptoms in<br>individuals with a history of mental health concerns are due to a functional disorder when organic<br>pathology may be the sole cause|\n|From Bordini BJ, Stephany<br>jpeds.2017.02.065.|A, Kliegman R. Overcoming Diagnostic Errors in Medical Practice._J Pediatr._2017;185:19-25.e1.https://doi.org/10.1016/j.|\n\n\nIn **context-related biases** , the setting of the diagnostic evaluation influences how clinicians perceive and process the information used in medical reasoning (Table 1.3). At the level of the individual clinician, context can introduce bias by consciously or subconsciously causing clinicians to de-emphasize relevant information and amplify impertinent information. Important examples of context-related biases include the **availability bias** , in which comparisons to other recent patients influence the diagnostic reasoning for the current patient. For example, an infant in respiratory distress secondary to decompensated heart failure in the setting of unrecognized congenital heart disease may be erroneously diagnosed with bronchiolitis when presenting to the emergency department in the middle of a busy viral season. The **framing effect** introduces bias when the setting or manner in which a patient is presented influences how information is perceived. A patient with abdominal pain may have different differential diagnoses generated for their pain depending on whether they are evaluated in an \n\nemergency department, a primary care office, a gynecology clinic, or a gastroenterology clinic. Individuals with rare diseases who are evaluated in fragmented specialty settings are often susceptible to their clinicians **thinking in silo** , in which concerns are considered only within the spectrum of pathophysiology relevant to that specialty. Under these circumstances, no one individual clinician appreciates the comprehensive sum of the patient’s symptoms and findings, and a unifying diagnosis is not considered. \n\nFor individuals with rare diseases who present acutely or critically ill, efforts to stabilize abnormal vital signs or laboratory parameters may succeed despite the lack of an accurate diagnosis, leading to an **outcome bias** and **hindsight bias** in which clinicians overestimate the efficacy of their diagnostic reasoning and therapeutic interventions. In these circumstances, the underlying illness has neither been identified nor specifically treated and the possibility of ongoing disease is no longer appreciated. Common examples include the failure to consider or comprehensively evaluate\n\n<!-- p.27 -->\n\n###### **TABLE 1.3 Cognitive Biases Related to Errors of Context** \n\n|Bias|Definition|\n|---|---|\n|Availability bias|Basing decisions on the most recent patient with similar symptoms, preferentially recalling recent and<br>more common diseases.|\n|Base-rate neglect|Over-or underestimating the prevalence of a disease, typically overestimating the prevalence of common<br>diseases and underestimating the prevalence of rare diseases.|\n|Framing effect|Being influenced by how or by whom a problem is described, or by the setting in which the evaluation<br>takes place.|\n|Frequency bias|Believing that common things happen commonly and are usually benign in general practice.|\n|Hindsight bias|Reinforcing diagnostic errors once a diagnosis is discovered in spite of these errors. May lead to a<br>clinician overestimating the efficacy of his or her clinical reasoning and may reinforce ineffective<br>techniques.|\n|Posterior probability<br>error|Considering the likelihood of a particular diagnosis in light of a patient’s prior or chronic illnesses. New<br>headaches in a patient with a history of migraines may in fact be a tumor.|\n|Sutton’s slip|Ignoring alternate explanations for “obvious” diagnoses (Sutton’s law is that one should first consider the<br>obvious).|\n|Thinking in silo|Restricting diagnostic considerations to a particular specialty or organ system. Each discipline has a set<br>of diseases within its comfort zone, which reduces diagnostic flexibility or team-based communication.|\n|Zebra retreat|Lacking conviction to pursue rare disorders even when suggested by evidence.|\n\n\nFrom Bordini BJ, Stephany A, Kliegman R. Overcoming Diagnostic Errors in Medical Practice. _J Pediatr._ 2017;185:19-25.e1. https://doi.org/10.1016/j. jpeds.2017.02.065. \n\nfor secondary causes of hypertension or chronic headache, leading disorders such as pheochromocytoma or a ruptured arachnoid cyst not to be diagnosed in a timely fashion. Furthermore, patients may improve in spite of medical intervention and not because of it, leading to the risk of clinicians reinforcing erroneous reasoning under the assumption that it was correct and directly correlated with clinical improvement. \n\nFurther compounding these diagnostic challenges are the dynamically interacting components of the **sociotechnical systems** in which clinicians diagnose and the ways in which they can confound diagnosis. The individual clinician does not operate in isolation but rather is one of many people in the healthcare environment _work system_ interacting with a variety of tools and technologies in a rapidly changing physical space to execute patient care-related tasks, including but not limited to diagnosis. External variables—like patient volume, ambient noise, the requirement to don and doff personal protective equipment, and the relative usability of electronic health record interfaces, for example—act together with internal variables like sleep deprivation, hunger, and divided attention to produce an aggregate cognitive burden that can either positively or negatively affect the clarity and efficacy of diagnostic reasoning such that the same clinical information presented to the same clinician under different circumstances can result in vastly divergent diagnostic impressions (Fig. 1.1). \n\nTo address these diagnostic reasoning challenges, several individual and systems-focused solutions exist. Efforts have \n\nincluded incorporating the cognitive psychology of diagnosis into medical education to drive greater awareness and use of system 2 cognitive processes; implementing cognitive forcing functions such as **diagnostic time-outs** to check for sources of bias in the diagnostic formulation (Table 1.4); organizing healthcare teams to promote more collaborative approaches to diagnosis; structuring healthcare systems to minimize disruptive environmental and technical factors; building decision support tools into electronic health records; and using human factors-based approaches that optimize the diverse sociotechnical elements of the healthcare environment. Despite these aids, diagnostic errors are frequent, impacting up to 30% of adult intensive care unit deaths and up to 20% of pediatric intensive care unit deaths, and resulting in significant morbidity and mortality across the entire healthcare spectrum. Rare disorders present additional challenges to diagnosis by virtue of their rarity. \n\n##### **Rare Diseases and the Diagnostic Odyssey** \n\nRare disorders face unique barriers to timely and accurate diagnosis, including limited clinician recognition and knowledge. Certain rare disease phenotypes are well characterized and are rapidly recognized when presenting with typical manifestations; others are readily diagnosed based on newborn screening results or an established family history. Depending on the disorder, however, features may vary widely due to factors such as variable expression or may evolve over time in ways that confound diagnosis.\n\n<!-- p.28 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0029_01.jpg)\n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0029_02.jpg)\n\n\n- **Fig. 1.1** The diagnostic process as it occurs within the larger sociotechnical healthcare work system. The patient and diagnosing clinician exist as part of a larger system containing other members of the diagnostic team, all interacting with various technologies and tools in a dynamically changing environment working toward completion of the common task of diagnosis. Changes in any of these variables can positively or negatively impact the outcome. (Balogh, E.P., Miller, B.T., Ball, J.R., 2015. _Improving Diagnosis in Health Care_ . National Academies Press, Washington, DC.) \n\n###### **TABLE Examples of Diagnostic Time-Out Checklists for (A) General Medical Practice and (B) Rare Disease 1.4 Evaluations** \n\n###### **TABLE** \n\n###### **A. General Medical Diagnostic Time-Out Checklist** \n\n- Name the clinical concern or diagnostic dilemma \n\n- Remove diagnostic labels and instead list out signs and symptoms \n\n- Ask—“Do we currently have a leading diagnosis?” If so, \n\n   - What clinical data cannot be explained with the provisional diagnosis? \n\n   - What are the “cannot miss” or “worst case scenario” diagnoses? \n\n- Broaden the differential using an anatomic or age-based approach \n\n- Decide on next steps: \n\n   - Obtain further history and repeat physical exam \n\n   - Review labs and actual images (not just the reports) \n\n   - Discuss with other team members (subspecialists, consultants, nurses) and family \n\n   - Obtain further labs and imaging \n\n###### **B. Rare Disease Evaluation Diagnostic Time-Out Checklist** \n\n1. Have we gathered sufficient information upon which to base our analysis? \n\n2. Have we unpacked that information and processed it in an objective and bias-free manner? \n\n3. What is the ultimate phenotype suggested by the patient’s complaints and findings? \n\n4. Does that phenotype suggest one underlying disease process or multiple disease processes occurring simultaneously? \n\n5. Have we generated plausible hypotheses regarding the pathophysiology of these complaints and findings? \n\n6. Have we implemented a directed diagnostic testing strategy using sufficiently sensitive and specific diagnostic assays that will allow us to discern among these diagnostic hypotheses in a Bayesian probabilistic fashion, as opposed to a dichotomous, “rule in, rule out” fashion? \n\n7. Have we checked for additional sources of bias? \n\n8. Have we considered alternate diagnoses sufficiently, and in particular, “do-not-miss” diagnoses? \n\nFrom Yale S, Cohen S, Bordini BJ. Diagnostic time-outs to improve diagnosis. _Crit Care Clin._ 2022;38(2):185-194. And from Bordini BJ, Kliegman RM, Basel D, Nocton J. Undiagnosed and rare diseases in perinatal medicine: lessons in context and cognitive diagnostic error. _Clin Perinatol._ 2020;47(1):1-14.\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0028_01.jpg)\n\n\n<!-- p.29 -->\n\nIn these instances, this **ascertainment bias** arises when an individual’s findings fall outside the spectrum of features that were present in the patient cohorts upon which disease descriptions were initially based, leading that individual’s findings to appear inconsistent with the supposedly established phenotype. Other rare disorders remain undiagnosed for extended periods of time, though not by virtue of a lack of clinician familiarity with symptoms, but rather because the disease has yet to be identified. In other instances, symptoms closely mimic more common disorders, obscuring diagnosis. Not all patients with unexplained symptoms have a rare disease. Some have atypical manifestations of a common disorder, while others may have more than one underlying common disease process occurring simultaneously, with the resultant constellation of symptoms confounding diagnosis. \n\nRegardless of how symptoms manifest, one theme is common: individuals with rare disorders often find themselves on a **diagnostic odyssey** , the journey of developing symptoms, seeking evaluation, experiencing symptom evolution, and seeking further evaluation, all in the attempt to obtain an accurate diagnosis. The diagnostic odyssey can last months, years, or even decades, and can be thought of as occurring in several distinct phases (Fig. 1.2). First, as symptoms develop, individuals or their families start to notice the findings that will ultimately be recognized as manifestations of the underlying disease. When those symptoms persist, most individuals first seek evaluation from their primary care provider, where routine testing may fail to yield conclusive evidence of an underlying disorder, or where a diagnosis may be established. If evaluation with the primary care provider fails to reveal a diagnosis, individuals are typically referred to a specialist based on the involved organ system suggested by the symptoms. For some, specialty evaluation results in a diagnosis, and the odyssey ends. For others, referrals to additional specialists—some of whom may be explicitly focused on evaluating for rare disorders or may have deep knowledge or experience diagnosing and treating rare conditions without necessarily \n\nbeing considered rare disease experts—are required, but there can be many delays, detours, and disruptions along the path to diagnosis, and despite best intentions, individuals may remain undiagnosed after months or even years of evaluation. \n\nIn addition to limitations in clinician recognition and knowledge of rare diseases, as well as the confounding effects of diagnostic error, the diagnostic odyssey may be prolonged due to impaired access to diagnosis. **Diagnostic access** is the ability to be evaluated in a healthcare environment with the requisite knowledge, experience, and resources capable of producing a timely, accurate, and satisfactory explanation for patient signs and symptoms. Impaired diagnostic access can broadly be divided into three categories. First, some individuals may experience impaired diagnostic access because they remain in **diagnostic stasis** , engaging in a plan of symptom management that may result in the ability to function day-to-day, but without a specific diagnosis and properly tailored management plan. These individuals may persist without a satisfactory diagnosis because their symptomatic management plan is sufficient to allow them to continue living their daily lives, despite ultimately being suboptimal or improperly matched to their true disease process. These individuals remain within the primary and specialty care intervals of the diagnostic odyssey and fail to be referred for further evaluation of a possible rare disease. _These patients have a rare disease, but do not necessarily know it._ \n\nIn contrast, there are patients who know they are on a diagnostic odyssey, who are referred for rare disease evaluation, but who still experience impaired diagnostic access because they are not accepted for, are unable to participate in, or are in resource-limited settings that preclude the evaluation. Some may be unable to do so because their health, personal circumstances, or other social determinants of health limit the ability to be evaluated. Some may not have access to rare disease specialists in their geographic region. Others may not meet program selection criteria. Fortunately, many larger-scale rare disease evaluation \n\nDiagnostic odyssey \n\n|Patie<br>First<br>symptom|nt interval<br>P<br>First<br>presentation/<br>clinical<br>appearance|rimary care interv<br>First<br>investigation,<br>primary care<br>responsible<br>for the patient|al<br>Seco<br>Dia<br>First referral<br>to<br>secondary<br>care|ndary care int<br>gnostic interv<br>First<br>specialist<br>visit|erval<br>Ter<br>al<br>First referral<br>to rare<br>disease<br>center|tiary care inte<br>First rare<br>disease<br>specialist<br>visit|rval<br>Molecular,<br>functional,<br>or otherwise<br>confirmed<br>diagnosis|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0029_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 1.2** The diagnostic odyssey, from first appearance of symptoms to confirmed diagnosis.  (Adapted from Soomers V, Husson O, Young R, Desar I, Van der Graaf W. The sarcoma diagnostic interval: a systematic review on length, contributing factors and patient outcomes. _ESMO Open_ . 2020 Feb;5[1]:e000592. \n\ndoi: 10.1136/esmoopen-2019-000592. PMID: 32079621; PMCID: PMC7046415.)\n\n<!-- p.30 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0031_01.jpg)\n\n\nprograms provide assistance with travel, lodging, and evaluation costs to ameliorate these barriers (see Chapter 3). Furthermore, many rare disease programs have developed virtual referral networks or have increased their capacity to engage in distance-based consultation. Nonetheless, many patients on a diagnostic odyssey are still unable to satisfactorily obtain timely or comprehensive evaluation. \n\nThere are patients with rare or undiagnosed diseases who present acutely or critically ill and require emergent care due to physiological instability. Depending on the severity of their illness and the healthcare setting in which they are being treated, they may not have adequate access to a team of expert diagnosticians that can mobilize rapidly around them, where they are, and evaluate them directly at the bedside. The lack of such resources presents an additional point of impaired diagnostic access. \n\nAdequate diagnostic access consists of the relative availability of disease-specific knowledge to patients and clinicians, as well as the affordability or availability of advanced diagnostic modalities. However, access to information and advanced diagnostic technologies alone is not enough. With increasing parity in terms of access to diagnostic technology, many primary care or specialty clinicians can order advanced diagnostic testing for their patients when facing a diagnostic dilemma. Patients often present for evaluation to a rare disease specialist already having obtained at least one or more gene panels, if not having genetic sequencing already completed, and yet persisting without a diagnosis. Next-generation molecular genetic technologies, if not paired with high-quality phenotypic data, may not only fail to attain diagnosis, but can also confound the diagnostic formulation. As such, the final critical component of diagnostic access is the availability of a coordinated and collaborative team of clinicians that can recognize the possibility of a rare disease and provide high-quality and refined phenotyping to inform the ordering and interpretation of advanced diagnostic modalities such as genomic sequencing. Despite best efforts with access to advanced diagnostics, approximately two-thirds of patients remain undiagnosed because there are still a number of disease states in which the molecular etiology is unknown and there are insufficient biomarkers to assist in making a clinical diagnosis. \n\n##### **When to Suspect a Rare Disease** \n\nMedical education is structured to provide clinicians with the ability to readily recognize the key historical and physical features of common disorders and to seek common explanations for patient concerns. While rare disorders are often included in discussions of symptomatology and pathophysiology, such discussions usually highlight the relative infrequency with which rare disorders are encountered, an approach exemplified by the medical education aphorism “ _When you hear hoofbeats, think of horses, not zebras_ ,” wherein horses represent more commonly occurring conditions and zebras represent rare disorders. Medical \n\ntrainees are frequently taught to synthesize differential diagnoses through some form of mnemonic which nearly invariably contains an _other_ category of less commonly encountered conditions, an approach that risks implicitly de-emphasizing their consideration. Even though rare diseases are seldom the primary diagnosis, they _should_ be considered when elements of the story do not fit the narrative of the presumed diagnosis or when patients fail to respond to therapy in an expected fashion. \n\nSuspicion of a rare disorder may arise at any time. Some rare disorders do present with pathognomonic historical, physical, laboratory, or imaging findings that allow for rapid diagnosis. In other circumstances, an established family history lends clarity to testing and management strategies for other family members. Other times, however, rare disorders present elusively or mimic more common disorders. The challenge lies in developing a properly calibrated sense of when patient features fall outside the spectrum of common disorders and instead suggest the possibility of a rare disease and the need to engage in more deliberate analytical diagnostic strategies. \n\nThere are several critical windows across the lifespan during which rare disorders may be more likely to present. Prenatal genetic testing or ultrasounds may reveal concerning findings; newborn screening results may return abnormal; early growth or developmental delays may become evident; previously acquired developmental skills may regress; childhood illnesses may be abnormally prolonged, more severe, or more frequent; and developmental hormonal changes, such as puberty or menopause, may trigger clinical suspicions. Other times, the onset of a rare disease may be sporadic, sudden, or severe. Individuals who present with acute-onset, unexplained critical illness, or those who have recurrent episodes of significant biochemical or neurological abnormalities without clear provoking triggers readily warrant consideration of a rare disease. Alternately, the progression of a rare disease may be subtle and slow; for example, neurological regression in the setting of a presumably static developmental disorder may suggest a progressive condition that was previously mimicking a diagnosis of an entity such as cerebral palsy. \n\n##### **Clinical Approaches to Rare Disease Diagnosis** \n\nGiven the wide heterogeneity of rare disorders, a single, unified, prescriptive approach to their diagnosis is not possible. Instead, there are several critical factors that when optimized can increase the likelihood that a rare diagnosis will be considered and evaluated in a timely fashion. First among these factors is _recognition_ . While medical education and rare disease advocacy efforts can increase awareness of specific disorders or classes of rare disease, with over 7,000 identified individual rare disorders, it is infeasible for any one clinician or team of clinicians to develop and maintain a comprehensive knowledge base of all rare disorders. Rather, these efforts of increasing awareness are best paired\n\n<!-- p.31 -->\n\nwith efforts designed to enhance **diagnostic calibration** , the sense of concordance between diagnostic confidence and diagnostic accuracy. A well-calibrated diagnostician is more likely to recognize when patient features do not align with a presumptive diagnosis and pursue alternate diagnoses and more analytic diagnostic reasoning. The same approaches designed to mitigate the risks of cognitive bias and cognitive diagnostic error, when applied to individual clinicians or evaluation teams, can enhance diagnostic calibration. Beyond the level of the individual clinician or evaluation team, rare disease recognition can be optimized via the incorporation of data sciences into clinical enterprises. Artificial intelligence paired with natural language processing in electronic health records can produce phenotype candidates based on selection and filtering criteria designed to recognize features that suggest the possibility of a rare disease. \n\nBeyond recognition, rare disease diagnosis is optimized when referrals are streamlined and inequities in access are addressed. Many individuals with rare diseases experience diagnostic delays and impaired diagnostic access due to insufficient rare disease-focused workforce resources prolonging the time to evaluation. Incentivizing and promoting rare disease specialization, while providing enhanced support for primary care providers to recognize and refer patients with possible rare diseases, can shorten these delays. Beyond the scope of diagnostic access lie the more universal topics of healthcare access and health disparities. Increasing representation of community stakeholders in discussions of rare disease resource allocation and advocating for rare disease efforts in broader policymaking decisions can more precisely match rare disease care to the communities that are underserved. The development of local rare disease evaluation teams within community-based and academic medical centers, along with the creation of regional, national, and international collaborative rare disease consortia, can mitigate these disparities further (see Chapter 3). \n\nThe next factor in optimizing rare disease diagnosis is ensuring that rare disease evaluations result in the generation of high-quality phenotypes. As access to advanced diagnostic modalities increases, improved diagnostic yields will become increasingly dependent on **high-quality phenotyping** , wherein a detailed list of objective physical and laboratory findings, semi-objective symptoms and findings, and subjective symptoms is generated and paired with data analysis tools that generate probability-matched descriptive terms using standardized systems such as the **human phenotype ontology** . These phenotypes can be used to generate not only a differential diagnosis, but also a **differential pathophysiology,** the list of potential underlying pathophysiological mechanisms that link seemingly unrelated findings together in a unifying diagnosis. High-quality phenotypes can also be matched with quantifiable laboratory data and genomic data to generate decision support tools which aim to limit bias and diagnostic error, as well as reduce the time to accurate diagnosis, thereby reducing the odyssey for patients impacted by a rare disease diagnosis. \n\nRare disease diagnosis is optimized when high-quality phenotypic data are paired with the appropriate diagnostic modalities. Next-generation sequencing-based approaches have diagnostic yields no greater than 50% and are more commonly around 30%. While phenotypic refinement and periodic sequencing reanalysis improve the diagnostic yield, many individuals remain undiagnosed after sequencing and may benefit from more advanced diagnostic modalities such as long-read sequencing, transcriptomics, metabolomics, methylation studies, pan-genome referencing, matchmaking, functional studies (see Chapter 2), as well as the consideration of non-genetic disease. \n"});
window.__registerChapter({"num": 2, "slug": "002_Diagnostic_Methods_Genomics_Omics_Biomarkers_Imaging_and_Other_Techn", "title": "Diagnostic Methods: Genomics, Omics, Biomarkers, Imaging, and Other Technologies", "authors": "Francesc Palau, Guerau Fernandez, Janet Hoenicka, Rafael Artuch, Jordi Muchart", "part_num": "I", "part_title": "Introductory Chapters", "word_count": 11367, "pearls": ["WES is considered the gold standard approach in clinical practice because it allows the reanalysis of novel genes compared to CES, generates a smaller amount of data, and is more affordable than WGS.", "Uniparental disomy can be due to abnormal genomic imprinting, which leads to recessive variant effects.", "In the clinical setting, genomic imprinting disorders can be diagnosed by specific MLPA tests determining the methylation profiling to determine the genes preferentially expressed from only one of the two parental chromosomes (i.e., Prader-Willi syndrome vs.", "Sometimes, imaging findings can be pathognomonic, but often they help narrow the differential diagnosis.", "Clinical applications in pediatrics are extensive, the most common in nephro-urology, oncology, and inflammatory diseases.", "Medical diagnosis is necessary for adequate clinical management of people living with a rare disease.", "Human cells have two dialoguing genomes: the nuclear genome (nDNA) and the mitochondrial genome (mtDNA)."], "genes": ["CFTR", "DMD", "FXN", "GDAP1", "GNB5", "NSD1", "PACS1", "PMP22"], "omims": ["#118220", "#152215", "#601002", "#611913", "#615009"], "subtopics": ["Diagnostic Methods Genomics, Omics, Biomarkers, Imaging, and Other Technologies", "Genetic and Genomic Testing in Precision Molecular Diagnosis", "Genetic and Genomic Testing for Chromosomal and Genome Abnormalities", "Genetic and Genomic Tests, Genetic Variants Detected, and Resolution Level", "Genetic Testing for Moderateto Small-Scale Gene Variants", "Genetic and Genomic Testing for DNA Single Nucleotide Variants", "Bioinformatics and Data Analysis", "Beyond the Exome and the Genome in the Diagnostic Testing of Undiagnosed Rare Diseases", "Primary Stages of the Genomic Bioinformatic Pipeline", "2.8", "FAIR Data Principles", "Transcriptomics", "The Epigenome: Beyond the Structure of the Gene", "Functional Genomics"], "figs": [{"img": "fig_p0036_04.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.1", "caption": "Genetic and genomic tests. Diagnostic rates (green) and resolution levels (red) are indicated for each test. bp, Base pair; CMA, chromosomal microarray; Kb, kilobases; LRS, long read sequencing; Mb, megabases; MLPA, multiplex ligation probe amplification; SRS, short read sequencing; SNV, single nucleotide variant."}, {"img": "fig_p0038_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.2", "caption": "Cytogenetic analysis of a syndromic patient with a chromosomal anomaly that affects chromosomes 4 and 11. The patient has a duplication at 4p14 and deletion at 11q25 due to meiotic abnormal segregation of the paternal reciprocal translocation between chromosome 4p and 11q. (A) G-banding karyotype of the patient with 46 chromosomes is observed, a derivative chromosome 11 (arrow) that contains duplicated material from paternal chromosome 4p and is missing chromosomal 11q material. (B) Paternal G-banding karyotype, 46 chromosomes, and a reciprocal translocation between chromosomes 4p14 and 11q25. (C) The patient’s CMA shows partial 4p14 duplication (left panel) and 11q25 deletion (right panel). (D) FISH study on a patient with 22q11.2 deletion syndrome: single locus probe TUPLE1 is absent in one of the chromosomes 22 (green); chromosome 22 is labeled with a specific probe (red). (Images courtesy of C. Hernando and E. Cuatrecasas, Sant Joan de Déu Children’s Hospital.)"}, {"img": "fig_p0039_03.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.3", "caption": "Chromosomal microarray analysis of genomic rearrangements or contiguous gene syndromes. Both panels show, from left to right, the ideogram of the chromosome, the genomic alteration in low resolution and the alteration in higher resolution, with an indication of the genes involved. (A) Microdeletion of approximately 593 kb on chromosome 16p11.2, associated with 16p11.2 deletion syndrome (MIM #611913), and (B) microduplication of 1.4 Mb causing the CMT1A form of Charcot-Marie-Tooth neuropathy (MIM #118220), which affects the expression of the myelin gene PMP22 . (Images courtesy of C. Hernando and A. Alcalá, Sant Joan de Déu Children’s Hospital.)"}, {"img": "fig_p0040_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.4", "caption": "Analysis of exon deletions and duplications by MLPA of the DMD gene. (A) The mother’s study shows that she has a 50% amplification of exons 46 to 50, which confirms who is a carrier of the deletion. (B) The son affected by Duchenne muscular dystrophy showing the outframe deletion of exons 46 to 50. (Images courtesy of L. Martorell, Sant Joan de Déu Children’s Hospital.)"}, {"img": "fig_p0041_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.5", "caption": "Dynamic variants due to expansion of microsatellite sequences. (A) Representative diagram of the genetic bases of neurological diseases caused by repeat expansions, some of them associated with genetic anticipation. The diseases are indicated according to the microsatellite sequence and its location in the gene. (B) Analysis of CAG exon expansion in Huntington disease (HD): left panel, capillary electrophoresis analysis of an HD patient heterozygous for 15 (normal allele) and 41 (pathogenic allele) CAG trinucleotide repeats (control individual is heterozygous for 13 and 14 CAG repeats); right panel, familial CAG repeat analysis using PCR DNA amplification, acrylamide gel electrophoresis, and silver nitrate staining: patient HD is heterozygous for alleles of 20 CAG (normal) and 47 CAG (pathogenic expansion), while the father is compound heterozygous (15 and 30 CAG repeats) and the mother is homozygous for the 20 CAG repeats allele; the 47 CAG allele is interpreted as a de novo expanded variant, probably originating from the paternal 30 CAG allele (false paternity and motherhood were excluded). (C) Analysis of the DMPK gene by TP-PCR of three DNA samples; (D) Southern blot study of the DMPK CTG expansion in a family with myotonic dystrophy type 1 (DM1), segregating the CTG expansion associated with genetic expansion: from left to right, lane 1, grandfather (early cataracts); lane 2, mother (classic DM1), lane 3: congenital DM1 in a newborn. ALS/FTD, Amyo"}, {"img": "fig_p0042_04.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.6", "caption": "Molecular analysis of pathogenic contraction of the D4Z4 repeat array in the subtelomeric region of chromosome 4q35. Southern blot of a prenatal diagnosis in a family with a D4Z4 23 kb rearrangement. The paternal grandfather is an asymptomatic carrier of the 23 kb variant after EcoRI digestion. In lane 3, a 20 kb band is shown after the EcoRI (E) and BlnI (B) double digestion, which reduces the 23 kb band by three kb, indicating that it corresponds to the locus on chromosome 4q35. In lane 4, the fetal DNA sample obtained by chorionic villus biopsy is analyzed. Observing the 20 kb EcoRI + BlnI band confirms that the fetus is a carrier of the pathogenic contraction on chromosome 4q35."}, {"img": "fig_p0042_10.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.7", "caption": "DNA-seq of a patient with PACS1 -related neurodevelopmental disorder (Schuurs-Hoeijmakers syndrome, MIM #615009). (A) Integrative genomics viewer (IGV) of the whole-exome sequencing by short-read sequencing (NextSeq500, Illumina), which shows the c.607C>T (p.Arg203Trp) variant in the PACS1 gene. (B) An electropherogram of Sanger sequencing confirms the presence of the de novo heterozygous C>T substitution variant (red arrow) that is not present in parents (blue arrows) . (Modified from Palau F, Hoenicka J. Genoma humano y análisis genético. In: Neuropediatría. Fundamentos Prácticos . Caraballo RH, Campistol J, González Rabelino G eds. Buenos Aires; 2022: 94-112, with permission from Editorial Médica Panamericana.)"}, {"img": "fig_p0042_11.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.7", "caption": "DNA-seq of a patient with PACS1 -related neurodevelopmental disorder (Schuurs-Hoeijmakers syndrome, MIM #615009). (A) Integrative genomics viewer (IGV) of the whole-exome sequencing by short-read sequencing (NextSeq500, Illumina), which shows the c.607C>T (p.Arg203Trp) variant in the PACS1 gene. (B) An electropherogram of Sanger sequencing confirms the presence of the de novo heterozygous C>T substitution variant (red arrow) that is not present in parents (blue arrows) . (Modified from Palau F, Hoenicka J. Genoma humano y análisis genético. In: Neuropediatría. Fundamentos Prácticos . Caraballo RH, Campistol J, González Rabelino G eds. Buenos Aires; 2022: 94-112, with permission from Editorial Médica Panamericana.)"}, {"img": "fig_p0046_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.8", "caption": "Evidence framework for variant pathogenicity. This matrix organizes each of the criteria by type of evidence category and by the strength of the criteria for a benign (left side) or pathogenic (right side) claim. BS, Benign strong; BP, benign supporting; FH, family history; LOF, loss of function; MAF, minor allele frequency; path., pathogenic; PM, pathogenic moderate; PP, pathogenic supporting; PS, pathogenic strong; PVS, pathogenic very strong. (Modified from Richards S et al. Genet Med. 2015, with permission.)"}, {"img": "vfig_p0045_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.8", "caption": "Evidence framework for variant pathogenicity. This matrix organizes each of the criteria by type of evidence category and by the strength of the criteria for a benign (left side) or pathogenic (right side) claim. BS, Benign strong; BP, benign supporting; FH, family history; LOF, loss of function; MAF, minor allele frequency; path., pathogenic; PM, pathogenic moderate; PP, pathogenic supporting; PS, pathogenic strong; PVS, pathogenic very strong. (Modified from Richards S et al. Genet Med. 2015, with permission.)"}, {"img": "fig_p0046_03.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.9", "caption": "Degrees of the pathogenicity of genetic variants."}, {"img": "vfig_p0045_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.8", "caption": "Evidence framework for variant pathogenicity. This matrix organizes each of the criteria by type of evidence category and by the strength of the criteria for a benign (left side) or pathogenic (right side) claim. BS, Benign strong; BP, benign supporting; FH, family history; LOF, loss of function; MAF, minor allele frequency; path., pathogenic; PM, pathogenic moderate; PP, pathogenic supporting; PS, pathogenic strong; PVS, pathogenic very strong. (Modified from Richards S et al. Genet Med. 2015, with permission.)"}, {"img": "fig_p0048_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.10", "caption": "Technologies and methods to diagnose rare diseases when ES is unrevealing. Many of these technologies complement each other, fill the missing gaps, and should be analyzed in an integrated manner. The selection between metabolomics, proteomics, methyl profiling, and immune assays should be guided by the patient’s clinical presentation and existing candidate genes identified through sequencing. Functional studies can be used to validate strong candidate variants or elucidate the underlying molecular mechanism of the disease after identifying the causative gene. ES, Exome sequencing; GS, genome sequencing; UN, unstimulated."}, {"img": "fig_p0049_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.11", "caption": "The number of genetic variants detected according to the massive DNA sequencing strategy. CES, Clinical exome sequencing (Mendelian exome); WES, whole exome sequencing; WGS, whole genome sequencing."}, {"img": "fig_p0049_06.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.12", "caption": "Variant calling pipeline. Different steps of the bioinformatic DNA-seq pipeline lead to the annotated variants. Intermediate file names are boxed. Bam, Binary Alignment Map; Fastq, text-based format for storing both nucleotide sequence reads and its corresponding quality scores; Fastq’ refers to the reads subjected to modifications once passed the quality control step. Vcf, Variant Call Format."}, {"img": "fig_p0052_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.13", "caption": "Use of DNAm array analyses in Sotos syndrome. Inactivating NSD1 variants causing Sotos syndrome have been associated with a specific genome-wide DNA methylation (DNAm) pattern. Sotos syndrome is characterized by phenotypic overlap with other overgrowth syndromes, and a definite diagnosis might not be easily reached due to the high prevalence of VUS that is identified in patients with a suggestive phenotype. (A) Taking advantage of the Sotos syndrome-specific DNAm signature, DNAm profiles in individuals carrying NSD1 VUS were determined, and an MDS plot was used to test samples (orange, solid circles) for the Sotos syndrome training cohort (red, solid circles) and a control group including 295 healthy individuals and subjects with rare neurodevelopmental disorders (blue, solid circles) . An independent control group used for testing is also shown (gray, solid circles) . (B) The plot for SVM probability scores based on a machine learning-based classifier shows classification results for the tested samples (VUS, n = 8, orange solid circles ; validation samples, n = 1, red open circle ), and the in-house control cohort (blue = training, gray = testing). MDS, Multidimensional scaling; SVM, support vector machine learning. (Images courtesy of Tartaglia M, Ferilli M, et al. Genes [Basel] 2022, with permission.)"}, {"img": "fig_p0053_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.14", "caption": "Flow diagram showing the process of the in-house Translational Diagnostics Program (TDP). (Adapted from Pijuan J et al. J Mol Diagn. 2021 and Yubero D et al. Int J Mol Sci. 2021.) Illustration generated with BioRender (https://biorender.com, last accessed September 1, 2023, with permission). HPO, Human phenotype ontology; LP, likely pathogenic; VUS, variant of uncertain significance."}, {"img": "fig_p0054_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.15", "caption": "Chromatography mass spectrometry studies in metabolomics. (A) Gas chromatography mass spectrometry: typical chromatogram of urine organic acids where a large number of peaks corresponding to individual metabolites (upper panel) is observed, which are identified by the mass spectrometer like a fingerprint (ion fragments); this fragment profile is compared with that present in a library (lower panel and black arrow ). The selected metabolite is pyroglutamic acid (the arrow marks its ion fragments). The patient was diagnosed with pyroglutamic aciduria (glutathione synthetase deficiency; GSS gene: MIM*601002). The panel indicates the difference between this patient (peak in black) and healthy control (very low peak in blue). (B) Chromatographic representation from a liquid chromatograph coupled to a tandem mass spectrometer: some plasma amino acids are measured (every single peak represents one different amino acid)."}, {"img": "fig_p0054_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.15", "caption": "Chromatography mass spectrometry studies in metabolomics. (A) Gas chromatography mass spectrometry: typical chromatogram of urine organic acids where a large number of peaks corresponding to individual metabolites (upper panel) is observed, which are identified by the mass spectrometer like a fingerprint (ion fragments); this fragment profile is compared with that present in a library (lower panel and black arrow ). The selected metabolite is pyroglutamic acid (the arrow marks its ion fragments). The patient was diagnosed with pyroglutamic aciduria (glutathione synthetase deficiency; GSS gene: MIM*601002). The panel indicates the difference between this patient (peak in black) and healthy control (very low peak in blue). (B) Chromatographic representation from a liquid chromatograph coupled to a tandem mass spectrometer: some plasma amino acids are measured (every single peak represents one different amino acid)."}, {"img": "fig_p0054_03.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.15", "caption": "Chromatography mass spectrometry studies in metabolomics. (A) Gas chromatography mass spectrometry: typical chromatogram of urine organic acids where a large number of peaks corresponding to individual metabolites (upper panel) is observed, which are identified by the mass spectrometer like a fingerprint (ion fragments); this fragment profile is compared with that present in a library (lower panel and black arrow ). The selected metabolite is pyroglutamic acid (the arrow marks its ion fragments). The patient was diagnosed with pyroglutamic aciduria (glutathione synthetase deficiency; GSS gene: MIM*601002). The panel indicates the difference between this patient (peak in black) and healthy control (very low peak in blue). (B) Chromatographic representation from a liquid chromatograph coupled to a tandem mass spectrometer: some plasma amino acids are measured (every single peak represents one different amino acid)."}, {"img": "fig_p0054_04.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.15", "caption": "Chromatography mass spectrometry studies in metabolomics. (A) Gas chromatography mass spectrometry: typical chromatogram of urine organic acids where a large number of peaks corresponding to individual metabolites (upper panel) is observed, which are identified by the mass spectrometer like a fingerprint (ion fragments); this fragment profile is compared with that present in a library (lower panel and black arrow ). The selected metabolite is pyroglutamic acid (the arrow marks its ion fragments). The patient was diagnosed with pyroglutamic aciduria (glutathione synthetase deficiency; GSS gene: MIM*601002). The panel indicates the difference between this patient (peak in black) and healthy control (very low peak in blue). (B) Chromatographic representation from a liquid chromatograph coupled to a tandem mass spectrometer: some plasma amino acids are measured (every single peak represents one different amino acid)."}, {"img": "fig_p0056_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.16", "caption": "A pictorial review of the different imaging modalities. (A) Chest X-ray: postsurgical X-ray of a neonate with truncus arteriosus. The heart’s widening and abnormal shape, together with basal left lung opacities. (B) Echocardiography: morphology and function synchronized with the ECG can be assessed accurately. (C) Brain CT: 10 years patient with Crouzon syndrome. Axial CT with bone algorithm and window shows detailed abnormalities of the bone. 3D volume renders reconstruction. (D) shows complex craniosynostosis, surgical changes, and ventriculoperitoneal shunting catheter. (E) Brain MRI: axial T2DRIVE sequence, same patient as in C and D. The right cochlea (yellow arrow) and cochlear nerve (blue arrow) are easily identified. (F and G) Brain MRI T2* perfusion: relative Cerebral Blood Flow map (F) and quantification (G). The red-colored nodule shows very high perfusion, consistent with relapse in a patient with ependymoma. Values can be measured; the orange line is normal perfusion in the contralateral hemisphere, and the blue line corresponds to the lesion. (H) PET-DOTA: same patient as in F and G. High uptake is consistent with high amino acid metabolism and thus relapse. (I) PET-DOTA fusion with the CT scan."}, {"img": "fig_p0057_07.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.17", "caption": "Bronchial carcinoid in a 13-year-old female with incidentally discovered right lower lobe mass on a chest radiograph obtained for evaluation of cough and pneumonia. (A–C) Maximum-intensity projection coronal (A), fused coronal (B), and fused axial (C) images from a Ga-Dotatate positron emission tomography (PET)/CT scan demonstrate marked tracer localization near the right hilum (arrow) , with maximum standardized uptake value (SUVmax) = 47.6, consistent with a neuroendocrine tumor. This was resected and was confirmed to be a carcinoid tumor."}, {"img": "fig_p0058_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.18", "caption": "Bilateral putaminal nuclei in a 19-month-old patient with Leigh syndrome due to the 8993T>G variant in the mtDNA. An elevated inverted lactate is found at this echo time (135 ms) acquired on a 1.5 T Avanto scanner (Siemens Medical System, TE = 135, TR = 1.5 s, 16 16 matrix, interpolated to 32 32, 16 cm)."}, {"img": "fig_p0059_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 2.19", "caption": "Morphometric quantitative brain MRI analysis. (A) Axial 3D T1 with segmentation and labeling with FreeSurfer (Laboratory for Computational Neuroimaging, Athinoula A. Martinos Center for Biomedical Imaging, Harvard Medical School). This approach allows the quantification of many different metrics of the brain tissue, such as cortical thickness maps (B, in mm) and surface representation (C). (D and E) Patient with mucopolysaccharidosis type III, at 3 and 4 years old, without evident visual changes. However, with this quantitative approach (F, in mm ) we can clearly see a global loss of gray matter, specifically cortical volume."}], "tables_meta": [{"label": "TABLE 2.1", "title": "#### 2.1 Types of Genomic and Genetic Variants"}, {"label": "Table 2.2", "title": "Gen etic Variants"}, {"label": "TABLE 2.3", "title": "Nomenclature f or the Si i ngle Nucleotide Variants Position to the Reference Molecule Sequence"}, {"label": "TABLE 2.4", "title": "Nomenclature an Type of Variant d Characteristics of SNVs and Gene SVs Gene and Protein Nomenclature* [Disease Example] Interpretation and C"}, {"label": "TABLE 2.4", "title": "Nomenclature an Type of Variant d Characteristics of SNVs and Gene SVs Gene and Protein Nomenclature* [Disease Example] I a —cont’d nterpret"}, {"label": "TABLE 2.5", "title": "Filtering Elem ents to Identify the Pathogenicity of a Genetic Variant"}, {"label": "TABLE 2.7", "title": "### Beyond the Exome and the Genome in the Diagnostic Testing of Undiagnosed Rare Diseases"}, {"label": "Table 2.8", "title": "Software URL"}, {"label": "Table 2.9", "title": "Database Description URL"}, {"label": "TABLE 2.10", "title": "### FAIR Data Principles"}, {"label": "Table 2.11", "title": "Disease Categories Main Laboratory Biomarkers in Biological Fluids (Blood, Urine, and Cerebrospinal Fluid)"}], "body_md": "\n<!-- p.34 -->\n\n# 2 Diagnostic Methods Genomics, Omics, Biomarkers, Imaging, and Other Technologies \n\n**FRANCESC PALAU, GUERAU FERNANDEZ, JANET HOENICKA, RAFAEL ARTUCH, JORDI MUCHART** \n\nMedical diagnosis is necessary for adequate clinical management of people living with a rare disease. It is also the gateway for applying the appropriate treatment for each patient and developing new therapies, whether they are curative or allow to modify the natural history of the disease and improve the patient’s quality of life. This process can be very complex for patients whose symptoms suggest a rare disease and who have been searching for the appropriate diagnosis for some time. Clinical suspicion, based on symptoms and signs, often requires the analysis of biomarkers to confirm or rule out a diagnosis. Many rare diseases, especially inherited metabolic diseases, have detectable biochemical biomarkers in accessible specimens such as blood (plasma, serum, cells), urine, saliva, or cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). Markers from radiological or diagnostic imaging tests offer an accurate diagnosis of the disease or an organic structural alteration relevant to the clinical and therapeutic management of the patient. However, many rare diseases do not have biomarkers or imaging markers to reach an etiological diagnosis or to assess the individual’s physiological state and response to treatment. Incorporating genomics and other omics approaches into the clinical setting promises to reverse these difficulties and improve the ability to obtain a diagnosis. \n\nKnowledge of the human genome and its comparison between individuals has revealed that humans differ in at least 0.1% of the nucleotide sequence of their genome. These genetic variants can be polymorphic if present in more than 1% of the population, or they can be rare and low frequency. Variations in the human genome can be found in genes that code for proteins, those that code for noncoding RNA, and in regulatory regions; most are found in noncoding, intragenic, or especially intergenic regions. In clinical terms, much attention has focused on those variations that affect the protein-coding regions and are presumed to be mostly disease-causing. \n\n## **Genetic and Genomic Testing in Precision Molecular Diagnosis** \n\nHuman cells have two dialoguing genomes: the nuclear genome (nDNA) and the mitochondrial genome (mtDNA). The complete haploid nDNA has approximately 3.3 billion nucleotides or base pairs (bp), containing approximately 21,300 protein-coding genes and at least 25,000 noncoding RNA genes. The mtDNA has approximately 16.5 kb encoding 37 genes. Genetic variants range from changes affecting a single nucleotide base pair to those involving several kilobases (kb) or megabases (Mb) and even an entire chromosome (Table 2.1). In the diagnostic process, studying these variants requires applying genetic and genomic tests with different levels of resolution. \n\nGenetic diagnosis is the process of attempting to confirm or rule out a suspected clinical diagnosis by applying genetic tests and methods (Fig. 2.1). To achieve the genetic diagnosis, the chromosomes, the genome, or specific genes (sometimes also the gene product or function) are investigated to detect or exclude an alteration possibly associated with a genetic disorder. The genetic analysis looks for variations of the DNA sequences (also of the RNA) in the _germinal_ line or _somatic_ lines (e.g., tumors or postzygotic mosaics in affected tissues). When investigating the genome to find out variants that may be the cause of a rare genetic disease, three issues must be considered: (i) the level of resolution sought based on the suspected diagnosis, (ii) the type of genetic variant to be analyzed, either structural variant (SV) or single nucleotide variant (SNV), and (iii) what genetic test or method must be applied to achieve the diagnosis (Table 2.2). \n\n### **Genetic and Genomic Testing for Chromosomal and Genome Abnormalities** \n\nThe study of **chromosomes** is carried out through (i) karyotyping, which uses specific stains such as Giemsa staining to\n\n<!-- p.35 -->\n\n##### **TABLE** \n\n#### **2.1 Types of Genomic and Genetic Variants** \n\n|Genetic|||\n|---|---|---|\n|Resolution Level|Genetic Variants|Definition|\n|Chromosome|Deletion|Loss of a sequence of DNA and chromatin from a chromosome<br>observed in the karyotype|\n||Duplication|Gain of a sequence of DNA and chromatin (duplicated) from a<br>chromosome observed in the karyotype|\n||Insertion|DNA material inserted in a chromosome from another chromosome or<br>exogenous source|\n||Translocation|Transfer of a segment of one chromosome to another chromosome|\n|Genome|Structural variants (SV) and cop|y number variants (CNV):|\n||• Microdeletion|Chromosomal deletion usually less than 3 Mb (below microscope<br>resolution)|\n||• Microduplication|Chromosomal duplication usually less than 3 Mb|\n||• Other SVs|Small insertions, other rearrangements|\n|DNA nucleotide<br>sequence|Single Nucleotide Variant<br>(SNV)|A single base pair change of the DNA sequence. When the allelic variant<br>is polymorphic, it is usually referred to as single nucleotide<br>polymorphism (SNP)|\n||Variable Number of Tandem<br>Repeat (VNTR)|Minisatellite DNA sequences of tandem arrangement of variable number<br>of copies, which are highly polymorphic|\n||Short Tandem Repeat (STR)|Microsatellite polymorphic locus consisting of variable tandem repeats,<br>mainly (TG)n dinucleotides, but also trinucleotides or tetranucleotides|\n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0036_04.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.1** Genetic and genomic tests. Diagnostic rates (green) and resolution levels (red) are indicated for each test. _bp,_ Base pair; _CMA,_ chromosomal microarray; _Kb,_ kilobases; _LRS,_ long read sequencing; _Mb,_ megabases; _MLPA,_ multiplex ligation probe amplification; _SRS,_ short read sequencing; _SNV,_ single nucleotide variant.\n\n<!-- p.36 -->\n\n|**TABLE**<br>**2.2**|\n|---|\n\n\n#### **Genetic and Genomic Tests, Genetic Variants Detected, and Resolution Level** \n\n|||Gen|etic Variants|||\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Genetic Test|Chromosomal<br>Abnormalities and<br>Rearrangements|Genome Structural<br>Variants (SV) and<br>Copy Number<br>Variants (CNV)|Single<br>Nucleotide<br>Variants<br>(SNV)|Dynamic<br>Variants<br>(STRs)|Resolution Level|\n|Karyotype (G-banding)|+|+/−|−|−|Chromosomes<br>(5–10 Mb)|\n|FISH|+|+|−|−|Locus-specific on<br>chromosomes|\n|MLPA|−|+|−|−|Locus-specific<br>(10–50 specific zones,<br>usually exons)|\n|Chromosomal microarrays<br>(CMA): CGH array and<br>SNP-array|+|+|−|−|Genome, except<br>repetitive zones<br>(aCGH 60K: 50–100 kb)|\n|TP-PCR and capillary<br>electrophoresis|−|−|−|+|Gene (number of<br>microsatellite repeats)|\n|Sanger (dideoxy) DNA<br>sequencing|−|−|+|+/−|Gene (bp)|\n|Gene panels and clinical<br>exome (CES, Mendelian<br>panel)|−|−|+|−|Exome of disease genes<br>(bp) [most included in<br>OMIM]|\n|Whole exome<br>sequencing (WES)|−|+/−|+|−|Complete exome (bp)|\n|Whole genome<br>sequencing (WGS)|−|+|+|+/−|Complete genome (bp)|\n\n\n_bp,_ Base pairs; _CGH-array,_ comparative genomic hybridization array; _CMA,_ chromosomal microarrays; _CNVs,_ copy number variants; _FISH,_ fluorescent in situ hybridization; _kb,_ kilobases; _Mb,_ megabases; _MLPA,_ multiplex ligation probe amplification; _OMIM,_ Online Mendelian Inheritance in Man; _SNP-array,_ single nucleotide polymorphisms array; _SNVs,_ single nucleotide variants; _STRs,_ single tandem repeats or microsatellites; _SVs,_ structural variants. \n\nrecognize the banding pattern of the different chromosomes in metaphase or prometaphase during cell division (Fig. 2.2A and B); (ii) chromosomal microarray analysis (CMA) (Fig. 2.2C), and (iii) recognition of a particular chromosomal locus with fluorochrome-labeled molecular probes using fluorescent in situ hybridization (FISH) (Fig. 2.2D). The karyotype can determine the presence of numerical (complete or partial aneuploidies such as trisomy and monosomy) and structural (balanced translocations, Robertsonian translocations, inversions, insertions) chromosomal abnormalities. \n\n**CMA** is designed to detect large-scale genome and chromosome rearrangements. The CMA or molecular karyotype provides information about SVs, either deletions or duplications of genomic regions, whose size varies between 100 kb and three or more Mb. The technology is based on hybridization between the patient’s DNA under study and multiple probes fixed on a solid support (chip), with distribution along the set of chromosomes, allowing a scan of each chromosome. In **comparative genomic hybridization (CGH) array** , relative copy number losses \n\nand gains can be detected by hybridizing the patient’s DNA with control DNA on such microarrays, which contain genome-distributed probes representative of each chromosome (Fig. 2.2C and Fig. 2.3). A reduction of the patient’s signal indicates the presence of a _segmental deletion_ , whereas the signal overrepresentation suggests the presence of a _duplication_ . Another type of CMA uses single oligonucleotide polymorphism (SNP) arrays that contain the two alleles of multiple SNVs distributed along the chromosomes and allows detection of uniparental disomy. \n\n**In situ hybridization** studies use probes that hybridize to _specific_ satellite sequences of the centromeres of the chromosomes in both metaphase and nucleus interphase, which can be helpful to determine the presence of trisomy or monosomy, such as trisomy 21 of Down syndrome or monosomy X of Turner syndrome. **Locus-specific molecular probes** are a diagnostic tool for recognizing microdeletions, such as 22q11.2 deletion syndrome with its phenotypic variants (e.g., DiGeorge syndrome, velocardiofacial syndrome [Fig. 2.2D]), Williams syndrome due to 7q11.23 microdeletion, or Prader-Willi and Angelman\n\n<!-- p.37 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0038_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.2** Cytogenetic analysis of a syndromic patient with a chromosomal anomaly that affects chromosomes 4 and 11. The patient has a duplication at 4p14 and deletion at 11q25 due to meiotic abnormal segregation of the paternal reciprocal translocation between chromosome 4p and 11q. (A) G-banding karyotype of the patient with 46 chromosomes is observed, a derivative chromosome 11 _(arrow)_ that contains duplicated material from paternal chromosome 4p and is missing chromosomal 11q material. (B) Paternal G-banding karyotype, 46 chromosomes, and a reciprocal translocation between chromosomes 4p14 and 11q25. (C) The patient’s CMA shows partial 4p14 duplication (left panel) and 11q25 deletion (right panel). (D) FISH study on a patient with 22q11.2 deletion syndrome: single locus probe TUPLE1 is absent in one of the chromosomes 22 (green); chromosome 22 is labeled with a specific probe (red). (Images courtesy of C. Hernando and E. Cuatrecasas, Sant Joan de Déu Children’s Hospital.) \n\nsyndromes as the consequence of a 15q11.2 microdeletion. Locus-targeted FISH probes are also used to detect oncogenic amplifications and translocations in rare cancers, such as childhood acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Prenatal diagnosis of chromosomal aneuploidies is performed through noninvasive prenatal testing (NIPT) of circulating fetal DNA in the mother’s blood by applying massive DNA sequencing technology. \n\n**Optical genome mapping** has advanced ways to analyze SVs, labeling DNA molecules with fluorochromes and subsequently linearizing them into nanochannel arrays to detect submicroscopic rearrangements. \n\n### **Genetic Testing for Moderateto Small-Scale Gene Variants** \n\nStructural variation affects moderate to small genomic regions, between 50 bp to several kb in length, in most cases \n\nwithin a gene. Two classes of these genomic variants are distinguished: (i) intragenic deletion or duplication involving one to several exons that may affect protein domains or generate a frameshift in the open reading frame of the gene, and (ii) dynamic repeat expansions within exons, introns, or the 5′ or 3′ untranslated regions of the gene. \n\n**Intragenic deletions and duplications** are typically analyzed by multiplex ligation-dependent probe amplification (MLPA), which allows exons to be amplified quantitatively. This technique allows the detection of intragenic CNVs in patients homozygous for autosomal recessive traits or affected males hemizygous for recessive X-linked variants but also heterozygous parents (Fig. 2.4). Another option to analyze gene dose is the _quantification of PCR products_ . Real-time PCR quantifies copies of the amplified gene segment. Still, it requires normalization with reference standards of a well-defined gene that does not show any structural variant, either deletion or duplication. Digital\n\n<!-- p.38 -->\n\nA B \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0039_03.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.3** Chromosomal microarray analysis of genomic rearrangements or contiguous gene syndromes. Both panels show, from left to right, the ideogram of the chromosome, the genomic alteration in low resolution and the alteration in higher resolution, with an indication of the genes involved. (A) Microdeletion of approximately 593 kb on chromosome 16p11.2, associated with 16p11.2 deletion syndrome (MIM #611913), and (B) microduplication of 1.4 Mb causing the CMT1A form of Charcot-Marie-Tooth neuropathy (MIM #118220), which affects the expression of the myelin gene _PMP22_ . (Images courtesy of C. Hernando and A. Alcalá, Sant Joan de Déu Children’s Hospital.) \n\nPCR, however, offers precise and absolute quantification of the product without normalization. \n\n**Unstable repeat expansions** , also known as dynamic variants, represent genetic variants of a single trinucleotide repeat sequence or, less frequently, other small repeats that are the cause of several monogenic disorders, most of them related to the nervous system (Fig. 2.5A). _Short expansions_ , such as exonic CAG triplet-repeats that are translated into polyglutamine tracts (Fig. 2.5B) (i.e., Huntington disease and some spinocerebellar ataxias), are generally analyzed by PCR amplification with 5′ fluorophore-labeled primers, capillary electrophoresis, and fragment analysis. _Large expansions_ , such as those associated with myotonic dystrophy, fragile X syndrome, and Friedreich ataxia, are detected by triplet-repeat-primer PCR (TP-PCR) (Fig. 2.5C). When precise quantification of the expansion is required, long \n\nPCR or Southern blotting are appropriate methods to apply (Fig. 2.5D). Southern blotting is the oldest molecular method to detect polymorphic or rare DNA variants; it is needed to investigate the 3.3 kb D4Z4 repeats at locus 4q35 for molecular diagnosis in **facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy type 1 (FSHD1)** . The disease results from a heterozygous contraction of the D4Z4 repeat units in the subtelomeric region of chromosome 4q35 (Fig. 2.6). \n\n### **Genetic and Genomic Testing for DNA Single Nucleotide Variants** \n\nAnalysis of point or few nucleotide genetic variants is performed by linear reading of the nucleotide bases of both DNA strands. The DNA sequencing (DNA-seq) method depends on the objective of interest and the automated\n\n<!-- p.39 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0040_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.4** Analysis of exon deletions and duplications by MLPA of the _DMD_ gene. (A) The mother’s study shows that she has a 50% amplification of exons 46 to 50, which confirms who is a carrier of the deletion. (B) The son affected by Duchenne muscular dystrophy showing the outframe deletion of exons 46 to 50. (Images courtesy of L. Martorell, Sant Joan de Déu Children’s Hospital.) \n\ntechnology available. The development of massive parallel sequencing or next-generation sequencing (NGS), with different technologies and chemistries, has made it possible to read the genome, allowing many genes to be sequenced at once. There are two broad approaches for DNA-seq: (i) sequencing of PCR-amplified DNA that produces _short-read sequences (SRS)_ (Fig. 2.7A) and direct sequencing of a single DNA molecule (nonamplified) with long-read sequences (LRS); (ii) the classic Sanger sequencing method (dideoxy), which allows detecting variants in small amplicons or PCR products separately and consecutively (e.g., exons, gene fragments) (Fig. 2.7B). Regardless of the sequencing method, the description of the SNVs follows the same reference genome (Table 2.3) and the same terminology to indicate the change of the gene and product (Table 2.4). \n\n**Next-generation sequencing** technology can be used to detect variants by interrogating _gene panels_ encompassing a spectrum from under a hundred to several thousand proteincoding genes, the set of gene exons in the genome _(whole exome sequencing, WES)_ , or the entire genome _(whole genome sequencing, WGS)_ . Gene panels are generally designed to detect protein-coding variants of genes related to a specific group of diseases or phenotypes (e.g., lists of genes in the Genomics England PanelApp, such as intellectual disability or muscular dystrophies, at https://panelapp.genomicsengland.co.uk/panels/). A large non-phenotypically targeted panel are the clinical or Mendelian exome panels (CES) \n\ncontaining between 5,000 and 7,000 genes already associated with clinical disorders and MIM number (http://www.omim. org). In general for undiagnosed patients, WES/WGS performs better than targeted gene panels. \n\nOne of the challenges of genome sequencing by NGS technology is the large number of variants detected and the need to establish the pathogenicity of candidate causative variants. Pathogenicity assessment requires limiting the eligible variants after filtering according to frequency (distinguishing rare variants, common variants, and polymorphisms), genetic inheritance, interspecies evolutionary conservation (genomic constraint), and pathological precedents and reports (Table 2.5). After narrowing down the candidate variant by applying these filters, predicting the pathogenicity of a variant requires additional criteria. The American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics (ACMG) and the Association of Molecular Pathology (AMP) Committee reported guidelines and recommendations for categorizing the pathological effect of the variant and the combination of criteria to define such pathogenicity (Fig. 2.8). The categories and their specific standard terminology are “pathogenic,” “likely pathogenic,” “uncertain significance,” “likely benign,” and “benign” to describe identified variants in genes (Fig. 2.9). Notably, pathogenicity predictors have limitations. New efforts are needed to understand the biology of genetic variation and its effects on disease pathophysiology to improve the diagnostic capacity of genomic analysis.\n\n<!-- p.40 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0041_02.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.5** Dynamic variants due to expansion of microsatellite sequences. (A) Representative diagram of the genetic bases of neurological diseases caused by repeat expansions, some of them associated with genetic anticipation. The diseases are indicated according to the microsatellite sequence and its location in the gene. (B) Analysis of CAG exon expansion in Huntington disease (HD): left panel, capillary electrophoresis analysis of an HD patient heterozygous for 15 (normal allele) and 41 (pathogenic allele) CAG trinucleotide repeats (control individual is heterozygous for 13 and 14 CAG repeats); right panel, familial CAG repeat analysis using PCR DNA amplification, acrylamide gel electrophoresis, and silver nitrate staining: patient HD is heterozygous for alleles of 20 CAG (normal) and 47 CAG (pathogenic expansion), while the father is compound heterozygous (15 and 30 CAG repeats) and the mother is homozygous for the 20 CAG repeats allele; the 47 CAG allele is interpreted as a _de novo_ expanded variant, probably originating from the paternal 30 CAG allele (false paternity and motherhood were excluded). (C) Analysis of the _DMPK_ gene by TP-PCR of three DNA samples; (D) Southern blot study of the _DMPK_ CTG expansion in a family with myotonic dystrophy type 1 (DM1), segregating the CTG expansion associated with genetic expansion: from left to right, lane 1, grandfather (early cataracts); lane 2, mother (classic DM1), lane 3: congenital DM1 in a newborn. _ALS/FTD,_ Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis/frontotemporal dementia; _CANVAS,_ cerebellar ataxia, neuropathy, and vestibular areflexia syndrome; _DM1,_ myotonic dystrophy type 1; _DM2,_ myotonic dystrophy type 2; _DRPLA,_ dentate-rubro-pallid-luysian atrophy; _EPM1,_ progressive myoclonic epilepsy 1A; _FECD,_ Fuchs corneal endothelial dystrophy; _FRDA,_ Friedreich ataxia; _FXS,_ fragile X syndrome; _FXTAS,_ fragile X-associated tremor/ataxia syndrome; _HD,_ Huntington disease; _HDL2,_ Huntington disease type 2; _MJD,_ Machado-Joseph disease; _OPMD,_ oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy; _SCA,_ spinocerebellar ataxia. \n\nOther challenges of genomic approaches using NGS technology in medicine are related to the capture of sequences that may be inefficient, reducing the detection quality of some genes. This limitation can occur in WES, which also interrogates some untranslated sequences, splice site boundaries, regulatory sequences, and sequences of \n\nsome microRNAs. WGS does not suffer the disadvantage of reducing the coverage due to missing sequences. WES is considered the gold standard approach in clinical practice because it allows the reanalysis of novel genes compared to CES, generates a smaller amount of data, and is more affordable than WGS. New approaches such as long-read\n\n<!-- p.41 -->\n\nsequencing are entering clinical laboratories, and other methods are available or are being developed (Table 2.6, Fig. 2.10), some of which are being incorporated into clinical medicine. \n\n## **Bioinformatics and Data Analysis** \n\nNGS is a high-throughput and cost-effective technology that can detect various types of alterations, including \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0042_04.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.6** Molecular analysis of pathogenic contraction of the _D4Z4_ repeat array in the subtelomeric region of chromosome 4q35. Southern blot of a prenatal diagnosis in a family with a _D4Z4_ 23 kb rearrangement. The paternal grandfather is an asymptomatic carrier of the 23 kb variant after EcoRI digestion. In lane 3, a 20 kb band is shown after the EcoRI (E) and BlnI (B) double digestion, which reduces the 23 kb band by three kb, indicating that it corresponds to the locus on chromosome 4q35. In lane 4, the fetal DNA sample obtained by chorionic villus biopsy is analyzed. Observing the 20 kb EcoRI + BlnI band confirms that the fetus is a carrier of the pathogenic contraction on chromosome 4q35. \n\nnucleotide polymorphisms, copy number variation, transcriptome abnormalities, gene fusions, and epigenetic modifications. In this genomic data-driven analysis era, bioinformatic tools are paramount for the comprehensive processing, storage, and presentation of detected variability within an individual’s genome. \n\nThe advancement of DNA-seq using NGS technology has revolutionized genomics research but has also introduced substantial challenges related to the storage and computational analysis of the vast data generated. The complexity of DNA-seq data formats, including raw sequence reads, alignment files, and variant call files, demands specialized storage solutions to ensure data integrity and accessibility. Computationally, the analysis of NGS data is resource-intensive and complex. Alignment, variant calling, and downstream analysis steps demand substantial processing power, memory, and time. Efforts are directed toward optimizing algorithms, developing scalable and parallelized workflows, and leveraging cloud computing and high-performance computing clusters. Controlled computational environments such as NextFlow are required to ensure reproducibility. \n\nEach step in the bioinformatic pipeline leading to the detection of genomic variants **(“variant calling”)** is fundamental to generating high-quality data. Considering the proportion of DNA targeted by NGS technology, the extent of identified variability demonstrates significant disparity (Fig. 2.11). DNA-seq enables the exploration of genomic variations within different sequence ranges for an agnostic diagnosis. \n\nVarious bioinformatic tools have been created to handle distinct analyses, and comprehending the limitations of each procedure is essential for establishing the boundaries of the analysis. The nature of the specific genomic variation under investigation, whether it involves SNVs, SVs such as substantial deletions or duplications, splice sites, or DNA \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0042_10.jpg)\n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0042_11.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.7** DNA-seq of a patient with _PACS1_ -related neurodevelopmental disorder (Schuurs-Hoeijmakers syndrome, MIM #615009). (A) Integrative genomics viewer (IGV) of the whole-exome sequencing by short-read sequencing (NextSeq500, Illumina), which shows the c.607C>T (p.Arg203Trp) variant in the _PACS1_ gene. (B) An electropherogram of Sanger sequencing confirms the presence of the _de novo_ heterozygous C>T substitution variant _(red arrow)_ that is not present in parents _(blue arrows)_ .  (Modified from Palau F, Hoenicka J. Genoma humano y análisis genético. In: _Neuropediatría. Fundamentos Prácticos_ . Caraballo RH, Campistol J, González Rabelino G eds. Buenos Aires; 2022: 94-112, with permission from Editorial Médica Panamericana.)\n\n<!-- p.42 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 2.3** Nomenclature f or the Si i ngle Nucleotide Variants Position to the Reference Molecule Sequence\n\n|Numbering Scheme*<sup>,†</sup>|Prefix|Numbering System|\n|---|---|---|\n|**Genomic DNA**|g.|Genomic reference sequence nucleotide numbering is g.1, g.2, g.3, …, etc., from the first to<br>the last nucleotide of the reference sequence|\n|**Circular DNA**|o.|Circular reference sequence nucleotide numbering is o.1, o.2, o.3, …, etc., from the first to<br>the last nucleotide of the reference sequence|\n|**Mitochondrial DNA**|m.|Mitochondrial reference sequence nucleotide numbering is m.1, m.2, m.3, …, etc., from the<br>first to the last nucleotide of the reference sequence (special case of a circular genomic<br>reference sequence)|\n|**Coding DNA**|c.|Numbering starts with c.1 at the A of the ATG translation initiation (start) codon and ends<br>with the last nucleotide of the translation termination (stop) codon, i.e., TAA, TAG, or TGA<br>(based on a protein-coding transcript)|\n|**Noncoding DNA**|n.|Nucleotide numbering is n.1, n.2, n.3, …, etc., from the first to the last nucleotide of the<br>reference sequence (based on a transcript not coding for a protein)|\n|**RNA**|r.|Nucleotide numbering for an RNA reference sequence follows that of the associated coding or<br>noncoding DNA reference sequence; nucleotide r.123 relates to c.123 or n.123|\n|**Protein**|p.|Amino acid numbering is p.1, p.2, p.3, …, etc., from the first to the last amino acid of the<br>reference sequence|\n|*For diagnostic applications, it is<br>†Reference sequence added aft<br>(https://hgvs-nomenclature.org/|strongly rec<br>er Communit<br>stable/backg|ommended to use either RefSeq sequence (NCBI) or Ensembl sequence (EBI).<br>y Consultation by the HGVS Nomenclature version 21.1.3, update 2025-06-04<br>round/refseq/#reference-sequence-types).|\n\n\n\n#### **TABLE 2.4** Nomenclature an Type of Variant d Characteristics of SNVs and Gene SVs Gene and Protein Nomenclature* [Disease Example] Interpretation and Comments Percentage of Disease-Causing Variants\n\n|**Single Nucleotide Varian**<br>Nucleotide Substitutions<br>|**ts(SNVs)**<br>||<br>|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Missense|NM_000518.5_(HBB)_:<br>c.20A>T (p.Glu7Val)<br>_[Sickle cell disease]_|A single base-pair point variant (GAG<br>to GTG) results in the substitution<br>of the amino acid glutamic acid<br>(hydrophilic) to valine (hydrophobic)<br>in the 7th position of the β-chain<br>of hemoglobin referred to as<br>hemoglobin S (HbS)|<br> <br>40%|\n|Nonsense|NM_018972.4_(GDAP1)_:c.487C>T<br>(p.Gln163Ter)<br>_[Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 4A/2K]_|Premature stop codons|10%|\n|RNA processing variants|NM_000144.5_(FXN)_:c.385-2A>G<br>(acceptor site)<br>_[Friedreich ataxia]_<br>NM_016194.4_(GNB5)_:c.628-6G>A, which<br>creates a new alternative splicing<br>aggcagATCC site to the canonical site<br>gggcagATCC between intron 7 and exon 8<br>_[GNB5-related neurodevelopmental disorders;_<br>_Intellectual developmental disorder with_<br>_cardiac arrythmia]_|<br>Variants affecting RNA transcription,<br>processing, and translation: (a)<br>those involving splicing canonical<br>sites, either donor or acceptor;<br>(b) splicing substitutions that<br>create an alternative donor or<br>acceptor site|<br> <br>10%|\n\n\n_(Contiuned)_\n\n<!-- p.43 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 2.4** Nomenclature an Type of Variant d Characteristics of SNVs and Gene SVs Gene and Protein Nomenclature* [Disease Example] I a —cont’d nterpretation nd Comments Percentage of Disease-Causing Variants\n\n|Regulatory variants|||Rare|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Deletions and Insertions<br>|(delins)<br>|||\n|Addition or deletion of a<br>small number of base<br>pairs<br>**Gene Structural Variants**|NM_000492.3_(CFTR)_: c.[1522_1524delCTT]<br>(NP_000483.3:p.[Phe508del]<br>_[Cystic fibrosis]_<br>NM_018972.4_(GDAP1)_: c.862dupA<br>(NP_061845.2: p.[Thr288AsnfsTer3 or p.<br>Thr288fs])<br>_[Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 4A/2K]_<br> **(SVs)**|Three bp deletion within exon 10<br>with loss of the phenylalanine<br>residue in position 508 of the<br>CFTR protein<br>Duplication of adenine as a variant<br>that alters threonine 288 as the<br>first amino acid and shifts the<br>reading frame (frameshift),<br>replacing it with asparagine and<br>terminating after three more.|25%|\n|Large gene deletions,<br>duplications,<br>inversions, and<br>fusions|Intragenic complex mechanisms and<br>variable variant nomenclature:<br>e.g., intron 22 inversion of the_F8_gene<br>_[hemophilia A]_|Involving one or several exons and/<br>or introns|Rare|\n|Insertions or<br>rearrangements of<br>LINE or_Alu_<br>sequences|• Insertion of L1 element into exon<br>14 of the_F8_gene<br>_[hemophilia A]_<br>• Deletion caused by intrastrand recombination<br>between two repetitive sequences of the_Alu_<br>family in the_LDLR_gene<br>_[familial hypercholesterolemia]_|<br>Variants caused by repetitive<br>sequences|Rare|\n|Unstable repeat<br>expansions<br>(dynamic variants)|CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in<br>gene exon: (CAG)n, with n variable<br>_[Huntington disease; spinocerebellar ataxias_<br>_SCA1, SCA2, SCA3, SCA6, SCA12;_<br>_DRPLA]_|Exon, intron, 5′UTR, and 3′UTR<br>single nucleotide repeats that<br>may expand during<br>gametogenesis|Rare|\n\n\n*Specific transcripts (NM, NP) include NCBI reference sequences (RefSeq). Sequence annotation follows either GRCh37 or GRCh38 to define the genomic position. Matched Annotation from NCBI and EMBL-EBI (MANE) transcript represents an exact match in exonic regions between a Refseq transcript and its counterpart in the Ensembl/GENCODE annotation. \n\nnucleotide repeat expansions, governs the tailoring of bioinformatic pipelines and software tools. This tailored approach ensures optimal alignment with the unique demands posed by each variant type, thus enabling robust and accurate identification. Variations in sequencing depth and quality across the genome can introduce biases and complicate variant calling, potentially leading to false positives or missed variants. \n\nThe description of the four primary stages that are required in any given variant calling bioinformatic pipeline will highlight the importance of controlling every step of this process (Fig. 2.12): \n\n- _Quality Control:_ the sequencer generates fragments of a given length base by base. Each fragment is called a “read,” and each base in each read has a quality score assigned **(Phred Score)** . The initial reads are stored in a file called **Fastq** . In the quality control step, bases with low-quality reading and reads too short to be informative in the alignment step are removed. \n\n- _Alignment:_ once only high-quality reads are present in the Fastq file, these reads are mapped to a reference genome. The output file of this process is called the Bam file **(Binary Alignment Map)** . This file can be visualized using software such as Integrative Genomics Viewer (IGV; Fig. 2.7A). Reads are displayed throughout the reference genome, and differences between the sample reads and the reference genome are highlighted. \n\n- _Variant Calling:_ visualization tools such as **integrative genomic viewer (IGV)** are not variant callers, and specific software has been developed to determine when a variant is reliable and worth reporting. The file produced is called a Vcf file **(Variant Call Format)** . The most used tools for these first _three_ steps are listed in \n\n- Table 2.7. Multiple software tools have been developed for each step, and choosing which software is better suited for each task and their combination has critical implications for the final results. All callers detect most variation,\n\n<!-- p.44 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 2.5** Filtering Elem ents to Identify the Pathogenicity of a Genetic Variant\n\n|Filtering Criteria|Element Concept|Characteristics And Tools|\n|---|---|---|\n|Population frequency<br>(rarity)|MAF<1%, so it cannot be<br>considered a polymorphism<br>Data in the gnomAD database to see<br>the frequency of the allelic variant:<br>• heterozygous variant in dominant<br>disorders requires frequency in<br>controls to be less than the<br>frequency of the disorder<br>• biallelic variants in recessive<br>disorders must be less frequent<br>than the estimated heterozygous<br>carriers|Comparison between patients and controls, so it is present in<br>very few controls*<br>The Genome Aggregation Database is a resource of human<br>variation developed by an international coalition of<br>investigators, intending to aggregate and harmonize<br>both exome and genome sequencing data from a<br>variety of large-scale sequencing projects<br>https://gnomad.broadinstitute.org|\n|Expected inheritance<br>pattern of the<br>disease|Single gene disease, either<br>dominant or recessive, autosomal<br>or X-linked, or mitochondrial|The Online Mendelian Inheritance in Man (OMIM) offers a<br>comprehensive, authoritative compendium of human genes<br>and genetic phenotypes<br>https://www.omim.org|\n|Evolutionary<br>conservation<br>(genomic<br>constraint)|Criterion based on the concept that<br>natural selection maintains<br>sequences between species due<br>to their functional relevance; this<br>is especially relevant for highly<br>conserved amino acids that do<br>not tolerate nonsense changes|• PolyPhen-2: is a tool that predicts the possible impact<br>of an amino acid substitution on the structure and<br>function of a human protein using straightforward<br>physical and comparative considerations;<br>http://genetics.bwh.harvard.edu/pph2<br>• SIFT: Sorting Intolerant From Tolerant predicts whether an<br>amino acid substitution affects protein function based on<br>sequence homology and the physical properties of amino<br>acids;https://sift.bii.a-star.edu.sg|\n|Pathogenicity<br>prediction<br>_*_It is essential to take into acc<br>_MAF,_Minority allele frequency.|Predictor resources for interpretation<br>of sequencing variants and clinical<br>pathological impact<br>ount local population frequencies if available.<br>|•  ClinVar: information on the relationship between genetic<br>variants and diseases, with published evidence;<br>www.clinvar.com<br>• HGMD: the Human Gene Mutation Database collects<br>published pathogenic variants causing human genetic<br>disease;https://www.hgmd.cf.ac.uk/ac/index.php<br>• gnomAD: facilitates constraint monitoring analysis of<br>missense variants with pLI and Z-scores to indicate the<br>level of variation intolerance; and the pLoF program<br>measures the intolerance of a transcript to loss-of-<br>function variants;https://gnomad.broadinstitute.org<br>• Websites and programs for prediction of the biological<br>effect of variants. Some examples:<br>• REVEL: an ensemble method for predicting the<br>pathogenicity of missense variants based on a<br>combination of scores from 13 individual tools (freely<br>available);https://sites.google.com/site/revelgenomics<br>• Alamut Visual Plus: variant curation, annotation,<br>and interpretation along with missense and splicing<br>preditors;<br>https://www.sophiagenetics.com/platform/alamut-<br>visual-plus/<br>• CADD: the Combined Annotation Dependent Depletion<br>is a tool for scoring the deleteriousness of single<br>nucleotide variants as well as insertion/deletion variants<br>in the human genome;https://cadd.gs.washington.edu<br>• SpliceAI: a deep learning-based tool to identify splice<br>site variants by modeling mRNA splicing of genomic<br>sequence that allows prediction of noncoding<br>alternative splicing sites;<br>https://github.com/Illumina/SpliceAI|\n\n<!-- p.45 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0046_01.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0045_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 2.8** Evidence framework for variant pathogenicity. This matrix organizes each of the criteria by type of evidence category and by the strength of the criteria for a benign (left side) or pathogenic (right side) claim. _BS,_ Benign strong; _BP,_ benign supporting; _FH,_ family history; _LOF,_ loss of function; _MAF,_ minor allele frequency; _path.,_ pathogenic; _PM,_ pathogenic moderate; _PP,_ pathogenic supporting; _PS,_ pathogenic strong; _PVS,_ pathogenic very strong.  (Modified from Richards S et al. _Genet Med._ 2015, with permission.) \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0046_03.jpg)\n\n\n- **Fig. 2.9** Degrees of the pathogenicity of genetic variants.\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0045_02.jpg)\n\n\n<!-- p.46 -->\n\n#### **TABLE** \n\n### **Beyond the Exome and the Genome in the Diagnostic Testing of Undiagnosed Rare Diseases** \n\n|**2.6**<br>**y**|**g**|**g  g**|\n|---|---|---|\n|Omics Test|Characteristics|Applications|\n|Short-read<br>sequencing<br>(SRS)*|Reads typically 100–300 bp long; are mapped to<br>a consensus reference during the alignment<br>step|Exome and genome sequencing|\n|Long-read<br>sequencing<br>(LRS)|Improved alignment of very long sequences<br>to the reference genome|• Better detection of SVs, especially within repetitive<br>elements, segmental duplications, or high GC content<br>• Allows haplotype phasing of paternal or maternal<br>chromosomes<br>• Direct detection of methylated nucleotides|\n|Reanalysis|Periodic automated revision of sequencing data<br>with the latest analytical pipelines, variant<br>frequency databases, literature, and updates<br>in patient’s phenotype|Improve diagnosis rate with the already available exome<br>and genome sequences|\n|Pangenome<br>reference|A linear haploid consensus sequence of the<br>human reference genome representing the<br>diversity of human populations|Allows reads aligning to a pan-genome that considers<br>many alternate haplotypes at each locus, which reduces<br>the reference bias|\n|Transcriptomics<br>RNA-seq|/<br>Sequencing of gene transcripts with prioritization<br>and interpretation of potential disease-causing<br>variants and noncoding variants|RNA sequencing data with the analysis of expression<br>outliers, aberrant splicing, allele-specific expression,<br>and transcriptomic structural variants. Single-cell<br>transcriptomics allows for elucidating cellular and<br>molecular mechanisms|\n|Epigenomics|Epigenetic modifications like DNA methylation<br>and histone code modifications|Detection of epigenetic patterns of DNA: epi variants<br>(includes genomic imprinting) and episignatures|\n|Functional geno|i<br>mics||\n|**_in silico_**|From the genetic variant to the consequences<br>on the gene transcripts and protein functions<br>and structure|Prediction of the effect on splicing and pre-mRNA<br>transcripts and the impact on protein domains and<br>structure|\n|_in vitro_|Analysis of the cellular phenotypes and<br>biochemical interactions and functions|Functional biological studies in cell lines and patient’s cells:<br>lymphocytes, fibroblasts, iPSCs, organoids|\n|_in vivo_|Generation of disease models and organ<br>changes|Non-mammalian animal models: yeast_(S. cerevisiae)_, worm<br>_(C. elegans)_, fly_(D. melanogaster)_, zebrafish_(D. rerio)_|\n|Metabolomics|Metabolites profile in human clinical samples,<br>either targeted or non-targeted<br>metabolomics|Functional metabolomic studies may help to diagnose the<br>disease (biomarkers) and uncover the pathogenic<br>mechanism|\n|Proteomics|Tissue and cellular protein profiles that may<br>represent a disease state|Definition of known and new disease biomarkers|\n|Case matching<br>and<br>phenomics<br>networks<br>*It is the current stan|International databases, compendia, and<br>federated networks relating and supporting<br>phenotype and genotype relationships in<br>patients<br>dard NGS technology in clinical genetic/genomic laboratories.|Different actions and networks such as OMIM, Orphanet,<br>Matchmaker Exchange (genomic matching across<br>several databases (e.g., DECIPHER, GeneMatcher,<br>PhenomeCentral)|\n\n\nbut a small fraction of variability is only spotted by any individual software. This software specificity is due to the software’s fine-tuning options and the specific algorithms used. \n\n- _Annotation:_ once the variants have been detected, it is fundamental to know where they fall and the consequences at the genetic or regulatory level. Different databases that provide this information are needed so variants can be annotated and analyzed adequately by annotation databases (Table 2.8). Although DNA-seq is one of the most powerful tools for \n\n- detecting genetic variability, understanding the effects of \n\nthis variability at the organism level and detecting the causative pathogenic variant of a given disorder is not easy. In addition to the noteworthy rise in diagnostic yield (50% globally), NGS has amplified both the volume of generated data and, consequently, the level of diagnostic uncertainty. Interpretation of the biological significance of variants remains an ongoing challenge. \n\nWhile DNA-seq undeniably yields vast data, a more comprehensive approach to understanding a patient’s pathophysiology is necessary. Thus, a broader spectrum of information beyond genomic data alone must be incorporated while\n\n<!-- p.47 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0048_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.10** Technologies and methods to diagnose rare diseases when ES is unrevealing. Many of these technologies complement each other, fill the missing gaps, and should be analyzed in an integrated manner. The selection between metabolomics, proteomics, methyl profiling, and immune assays should be guided by the patient’s clinical presentation and existing candidate genes identified through sequencing. Functional studies can be used to validate strong candidate variants or elucidate the underlying molecular mechanism of the disease after identifying the causative gene. _ES,_ Exome sequencing; _GS,_ genome sequencing; _UN,_ unstimulated.  (From Marwaha S, Knowles JW, Ashley EA. A guide for the diagnosis of rare and undiagnosed disease: beyond the exome. _Genome Med_ . 2022;14:23 [Fig. 1].)\n\n<!-- p.48 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0049_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.11** The number of genetic variants detected according to the massive DNA sequencing strategy. _CES,_ Clinical exome sequencing (Mendelian exome); _WES,_ whole exome sequencing; _WGS,_ whole genome sequencing. \n\nfacing the patient’s clinical diagnosis. Integrating data from diverse sources, including imaging, wearables, other highthroughput omics technologies, and functional biology data, enriches the depth of analysis. However, clinical and phenotypic assessment is the cornerstone of this array of data. Genomic variability analysis must be anchored in a clinical hypothesis, guiding the exploration of the vast genomic landscape. _The understanding of genome variability demands the involvement of clinical expertise_ . The synergy between the clinical insights and the geneticists’ know-how is fundamental for an accurate diagnosis. Knowledge-based databases that relate genes to phenotypes, such as virtual gene panels or structured ontologies like the **human phenotype ontology (HPO)** , are crucial to enhancing this interaction. Experts in a specific field create virtual gene panels, while HPOs are generated by combining different databases and scientific \n\nliterature. HPO is a knowledge repository and a systematic framework for categorizing human clinical traits. Each distinctive abnormality or manifestation is tagged with a unique identifier and is assigned as a term within the ontology. These terms are intricately linked to genes that have been associated with the described phenotype. The hierarchical architecture of HPO allows for the finer categorization of phenotypes, creating a tree-like structured branching. In essence, the utilization of HPO ensures a standardized and unified language for articulating phenotypic traits, promoting precision and consistency across analyses. HPO also significantly facilitates seamless data-sharing collaboration among researchers and institutions. Through the concerted integration of clinical expertise, genetic exploration, and standardized ontologies, the genome becomes more decipherable and conducive to enhancing medical understanding and patient care. \n\nCollaborative efforts are needed to pool and disseminate valuable information for research, diagnosis, and treatment to overcome the challenges posed by the scarcity of data and resources for rare diseases. All the data generated from a given individual must be shared to increase the knowledge of a rare disease. These initiatives involve collecting, aggregating, and sharing various types of data, including omics data, clinical data, phenotypic information, and patient outcomes. Open-access platforms and databases encourage researchers to share their findings and insights, facilitating cross-disciplinary collaboration and the discovery of new disease mechanisms, biomarkers, and therapeutic targets. Patient registries, another essential component of these initiatives, enable the collection of longitudinal data, which is invaluable for tracking disease progression (natural history of the disease, not always adequately characterized in rare diseases) and treatment outcomes over time. All these data must be structured following principles that underpin the effective management and sharing of research and scientific data. These principles, known as **FAIR data principles** , are designed to enhance the usability and value of data across various disciplines. FAIR data is an acronym for Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable data (Table 2.9). \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0049_06.jpg)\n\n\n- **Fig. 2.12** Variant calling pipeline. Different steps of the bioinformatic DNA-seq pipeline lead to the annotated variants. Intermediate file names are boxed. Bam, Binary Alignment Map; Fastq, text-based format for storing both nucleotide sequence reads and its corresponding quality scores; Fastq’ refers to the reads subjected to modifications once passed the quality control step. _Vcf,_ Variant Call Format.\n\n<!-- p.49 -->\n\n|**TABLE**<br>**2.7**|\n|---|\n\n\n### **Primary Stages of the Genomic Bioinformatic Pipeline** \n\n||Software|URL|\n|---|---|---|\n|Quality Control|FastQC|https://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/projects/fastqc/|\n||Cutadapt|https://cutadapt.readthedocs.io/en/stable/|\n||Trimomatic|http://www.usadellab.org/cms/?page=trimmomatic|\n|Aligners|BWA|https://bio-bwa.sourceforge.net/bwa.shtml|\n||STAR|https://github.com/alexdobin/STAR|\n||DRAGMAP|https://github.com/Illumina/DRAGMAP|\n|Variant Calling SNVs|GATK|https://gatk.broadinstitute.org/hc/en-us|\n||DeepVariant|https://github.com/google/deepvariant|\n||Octopus|https://luntergroup.github.io/octopus/|\n|Variant Caller SVs|Manta|https://github.com/Illumina/manta|\n||Lumpy|https://github.com/arq5x/lumpy-sv|\n||ExomeDepth|https://github.com/vplagnol/ExomeDepth|\n\n\n### **TABLE Annotation Databases** \n\n#### **2.8** \n\n|Database|Description|URL|\n|---|---|---|\n|gnomAD|Offers insights into allele frequencies and genetic diversity across<br>different human populations|https://gnomad.broadinstitute.org/|\n|dbNSFP|Compiles predicted functional impact scores for non-synonymous<br>variants|https://sites.google.com/site/jpopgen/<br>dbNSFP|\n|CADD|Scores potentially pathogenic variants by considering evolutionary<br>conservation and functional annotations|https://cadd.gs.washington.edu/|\n|OMIM|It provides detailed information on the genetic basis of various diseases,<br>their clinical presentations, and molecular mechanisms|https://www.omim.org/|\n|ClinVar|Aggregates and curates clinical interpretations of genetic variants|https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/clinvar/|\n|ClinGen|It focuses on curating genes and variants relevant for genetic diagnosis|https://clinicalgenome.org/|\n|SpliceAI|Database that predicts the impact of genetic variants on RNA splicing|https://github.com/Illumina/SpliceAI|\n|DGV|Catalogues structural variations found in healthy individuals|http://dgv.tcag.ca/dgv/app/home|\n\n\n#### **TABLE** \n\n### **FAIR Data Principles** \n\n|**2.9**<br>**a**|**a rncpes**|\n|---|---|\n|Findable|This involves assigning unique and persistent identifiers to datasets, along with comprehensive metadata<br>describing the data’s context, origin, and relevance.|\n|Accessible|This involves employing standardized protocols and formats that facilitate seamless access and clear access<br>rights.|\n|Interoperable|This requires adhering to common data standards and formats, enabling different datasets and tools to work<br>cohesively.|\n|Reusable|This principle encourages data to be well-structured and adequately annotated, enabling it to be repurposed<br>for various studies.|\n\n\n_FAIR,_ Findable, accessible, interoperable, reusable.\n\n<!-- p.50 -->\n\n# **Transcriptomics** \n\nThe current knowledge about the genomic variability that occurs deep within the introns or close to the consensus sequences for identifying exon/intron boundaries is not sufficient to determine the origin of the molecular pathophysiology of a rare monogenic disease. The most widely used strategy to determine the detrimental effect of these uncharacterized genetic alterations is detecting transcriptomic abnormalities using RNA-seq. RNA-seq is a highthroughput technique that is able to characterize the _expression_ of all genes within a given sample. \n\nThree primary analyses are being performed to interlace genomic and transcriptomic data: \n\n- _Differential gene expression_ (DGE): When studying rare diseases, one of the most challenging problems is the availability of enough samples of the same kind to generate statistically significant results. Machine learning algorithms have made the comparison of one to many samples possible. These algorithms can determine genes significantly _up-_ or _downregulated_ from one sample compared to a reference cohort (usually representative of the same tissue of the studied sample). \n\n- _Allele-specific expression:_ Although genomic variability within one specific gene or genes might suggest that each allele comes from one parent, it might be possible that only one of both copies is being _expressed_ . **Uniparental disomy** can be due to abnormal genomic imprinting, which leads to recessive variant effects. \n\n- _Alternative splicing:_ Boundaries between exons and introns are very specific, and the process of cutting and pasting exons, which leads to the final transcript, is called splicing. The presence of variants in the consensus donor or acceptor splicing sites can cause the skip of critical exons or generate _alternative splicing sites (ASS)_ that could truncate the protein structure or function. When using transcriptomics to identify the molecular origin of a rare disease, the target tissue is not always available (e.g., the brain), and it is necessary to have a surrogate tissue that expresses the putative disease-causing genes. Another issue that might arise is gene expression variability within a given tissue. Transcriptomic studies use reference data sets that will be compared to the test sample. The composition of this standard cohort is fundamental to extracting nonbiased results; thus, it is essential to control the gender, ancestry, and disease health records of the different samples within this cohort. \n\n# **The Epigenome: Beyond the Structure of the Gene** \n\nEpigenetics refers to any factor that alters gene function without any change in the nucleotide sequence (the genotype). These modifications can be transmitted or heritable (from one cell to its daughter cell in cell division during mitosis and sometimes from generation to generation). \n\nEpigenetic changes to the genome determine which genes are expressed, in which cell type, and when. In cells, these modifications are stable and are transmitted in mitosis. The epigenome, therefore, can be defined as the global composition of chromatin (DNA and protein complex) that introduces patterns and marks in the genome, which regulate the functioning of genes (Chapter 4). \n\nEpigenetic patterns of DNA include _epivariants_ , which involve a change in the **DNA methylation (DNAm)** pattern of a small number of CpGs at a specific locus of the genome, and _episignatures_ , which are unique combinations of DNA methylation changes at multiple loci across the genome that are specific for different genetic syndromes. Episignatures are studied by genome-wide DNAm assay (Fig. 2.13). In the clinical setting, genomic imprinting disorders can be diagnosed by specific MLPA tests determining the _methylation profiling_ to determine the genes preferentially expressed from only one of the two parental chromosomes (i.e., Prader-Willi syndrome vs. Angelman syndrome at chromosome 15q12). \n\n# **Functional Genomics** \n\nThe widespread use of genomic testing leads to frequently detecting variants of uncertain significance (VUS). The functional study of the variant can unravel the altered molecular mechanisms and show abnormal biological patterns, supporting the causality of the disease. There are several clinical situations that may be solved with the support of functional studies: (i) genetic findings without sufficient evidence of pathogenicity, even being in genes already related to a clinical phenotype (i.e., OMIM); (ii) phenotype/ genotype incongruence, with new phenotypes associated with the gene; (iii) complex clinical phenotype with discrepancies or partial congruence with the genotype; (iv) suspicion of the presence of a second disease (comorbidity); (v) difficulty in the interpretation of the segregation studies of the genetic variant; (vi) difficulties in the interpretation of the inheritance pattern, which may be caused by a hypomorphic variant; and (vii) the gene candidate has not been associated with an OMIM phenotype, which suggests the presence of a novel gene. Functional studies can be addressed in _in silico_ , _in vitro,_ and _in vivo_ biological experiments (Fig. 2.10). To incorporate functional studies in the clinical setting, it is crucial to develop an in-house “Translational Diagnostics Program” to functionally validate candidate variants and genes (Fig. 2.14). It requires tight interaction and collaboration between the medical center and the associated research institutes and university departments. \n\n# **Metabolomics** \n\nMetabolomics encompasses studying small molecules (typically known as metabolites), lipids, or carbohydrates using different technical approaches. High-throughput analytical techniques are based on NMR spectroscopy or mass spectrometry coupled with liquid chromatography or gas\n\n<!-- p.51 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0052_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.13** Use of DNAm array analyses in Sotos syndrome. Inactivating _NSD1_ variants causing Sotos syndrome have been associated with a specific genome-wide DNA methylation (DNAm) pattern. Sotos syndrome is characterized by phenotypic overlap with other overgrowth syndromes, and a definite diagnosis might not be easily reached due to the high prevalence of VUS that is identified in patients with a suggestive phenotype. (A) Taking advantage of the Sotos syndrome-specific DNAm signature, DNAm profiles in individuals carrying _NSD1_ VUS were determined, and an MDS plot was used to test samples _(orange, solid circles)_ for the Sotos syndrome training cohort _(red, solid circles)_ and a control group including 295 healthy individuals and subjects with rare neurodevelopmental disorders _(blue, solid circles)_ . An independent control group used for testing is also shown _(gray, solid circles)_ . (B) The plot for SVM probability scores based on a machine learning-based classifier shows classification results for the tested samples (VUS, n = 8, _orange solid circles_ ; validation samples, n = 1, _red open circle_ ), and the in-house control cohort (blue = training, gray = testing). _MDS,_ Multidimensional scaling; _SVM,_ support vector machine learning. (Images courtesy of Tartaglia M, Ferilli M, et al. _Genes [Basel]_ 2022, with permission.) \n\nchromatography techniques, which separate metabolites. Some intrinsic characteristics of metabolomics studies differentiate it from genomic studies. Metabolomics are much more variable due to environmental factors such as diet, treatments, or the microbiome effect that can remarkably modify the metabolic profiles, even in the same individual, for a short time. Two approaches are currently being used in clinical laboratories to diagnose metabolic-genetic diseases, namely, targeted and untargeted metabolomics (Fig. 2.15). \n\n## **Targeted Metabolomics** \n\nThe targeted methods are the most universally employed for diagnostic purposes for rare genetic conditions in general and in inborn errors of metabolism in particular. This analytical approach is based on hypothesis-driven measurements of metabolites in body fluids, mainly in the blood (plasma or serum), urine, and cerebrospinal fluid. As an advantage, these methods are robust and allow absolute quantification of metabolites. Interlaboratory comparison quality control schemes (such as the ERNDIM program: www.ERNDIM.org; last accessed August 30, 2023) are available, with the final aim of harmonizing the different methodologies employed. Moreover, the deep knowledge accumulated over decades allows to screen healthy versus disease states and perform a biomedical metabolic profile interpretation. _Thus, although discovering new metabolites is unlikely, new associations of known_ \n\n_metabolites with diagnostic or prognostic features for different genetic conditions can be identified._ As a disadvantage, a limited number of metabolites can be analyzed with different dedicated methods. Typically, in a laboratory dealing with a diagnosis of inborn error of metabolism, around 30 different procedures to determine metabolic biomarkers are routinely used (Table 2.10). Because this approach focuses on targeted metabolites, no new biomarkers can be discovered, and it only provides a partial view of the metabolic landscape and its relationship with the genetic background. \n\n## **Untargeted Metabolomics** \n\nThis analytical approach is based on hypothesis-generating measurements by analyzing as many metabolites as possible in body fluids. Therefore, it has the potential to screen known and novel metabolites together and provide extensive information regarding overall genomic/environmental interactions. Thus, discovering new biomarkers for diagnosis or treatment follow-up purposes is possible. However, metabolite quantifications are less robust than the targeted techniques, and no external quality control schemes for interlaboratory comparisons are available. It is estimated that approximately 35,000 different “metabolic features” can be obtained after an untargeted metabolomic analysis. Bioinformatic pipelines are necessary to process and filter such features, and this number can be reduced to around 20,000\n\n<!-- p.52 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0053_02.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.14** Flow diagram showing the process of the in-house Translational Diagnostics Program (TDP). (Adapted from Pijuan J et al. _J Mol Diagn._ 2021 and Yubero D et al. _Int J Mol Sci._ 2021.) Illustration generated with BioRender (https://biorender.com, last accessed September 1, 2023, with permission). _HPO,_ Human phenotype ontology; _LP,_ likely pathogenic; _VUS,_ variant of uncertain significance. \n\nfeatures. Some of these features can be classified as _features of uncertain significance (FUS)_ . The untargeted metabolomics techniques are approaching maturity to be used in diagnosing and following patients with metabolic and other genetic diseases. However, they are only used for this purpose in a few highly specialized laboratories worldwide. Integration of metabolomics data in “multiomics” platforms (with phenotypic features, genomic, proteomic, and transcriptomic data, among others) is a challenge. International efforts will require integrating all these data in databases \n\n(e.g., The Human Metabolome Database, https://hmdb. ca/; last accessed August 30, 2023). \n\nOther promising tools to elucidate the pathophysiological basis of metabolic diseases are intracellular metabolomic analyses, which need to be fully standardized. Several critical methodological issues that may change metabolomic profiles have been identified, such as differences in culture media composition, cell harvesting, halting cell metabolism procedures, or the degradation of labile metabolites during metabolite extraction. Thus, one of the main challenges of\n\n<!-- p.53 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0054_01.jpg)\n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0054_02.jpg)\n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0054_03.jpg)\n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0054_04.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.15** Chromatography mass spectrometry studies in metabolomics. (A) Gas chromatography mass spectrometry: typical chromatogram of urine organic acids where a large number of peaks corresponding to individual metabolites (upper panel) is observed, which are identified by the mass spectrometer like a fingerprint (ion fragments); this fragment profile is compared with that present in a library (lower panel and _black arrow_ ). The selected metabolite is pyroglutamic acid (the _arrow_ marks its ion fragments). The patient was diagnosed with pyroglutamic aciduria (glutathione synthetase deficiency; _GSS_ gene: MIM*601002). The panel indicates the difference between this patient (peak in black) and healthy control (very low peak in blue). (B) Chromatographic representation from a liquid chromatograph coupled to a tandem mass spectrometer: some plasma amino acids are measured (every single peak represents one different amino acid). \n\nintracellular metabolomics studies is to reduce variability in the results due to technical factors. \n\n# **Proteomics** \n\nProteomics gives insight into the proteome’s composition, structure, and function, allowing the identification of specific signatures, mainly in cells. Thousands of protein species in a given cell type can be detected. Sensitivity is a limitation due to the extensive dynamic range of protein concentrations across sample tissues. Regarding biological \n\nfluids, the proteomic approach is one step behind the metabolomic one, at least from a diagnostic point of view. However, it has tremendous potential in discovering new biomarkers, as has already been demonstrated in different inherited metabolic diseases. Proteomic techniques are classified into untargeted and targeted proteomics. The general aim of untargeted proteomics is quantifying all proteins, leading to protein discovery and displaying advantages and limitations similar to those of untargeted metabolomic studies. Targeted proteomics is better suited to biomarker candidate verification and clinical applications.\n\n<!-- p.54 -->\n\n### **TABLE 2.10 Updated Inborn Errors of Metabolism Categories** \n\n|Disease Categories|Main Laboratory Biomarkers in Biological Fluids (Blood, Urine, and<br>Cerebrospinal Fluid)|\n|---|---|\n|Amino acid metabolism|Amino acids, organic acids, acylcarnitines|\n|Peptide and amines metabolism|Peptides, polyamines, amines, other|\n|Carbohydrate metabolism|Carbohydrates|\n|Fatty acid and ketone body metabolism|Acylcarnitines, organic acids|\n|Energy substrate metabolism|Organic acids, creatine.|\n|Mitochondrial disorders*|Organic acids, amino acids, acylcarnitines, cytokines, other|\n|Metabolite repair/proofreading|Organic acids, acylcarnitines|\n|Intermediary metabolism (miscellaneous)|Organic acids|\n|Lipid metabolism|Fatty acids, very long chain fatty acids, sterols, bile acids, complex lipids|\n|Lipoprotein metabolism|Lipid profile, lipoproteins|\n|Nucleotide metabolism|Purines and pyrimidines|\n|Tetrapyrrole metabolism|Porphyrins, heme|\n|Congenital disorders of glycosylation|Sialotransferrin, other glycoproteins|\n|Organelle biogenesis, dynamics, and interactions|Very long-chain fatty acids, organic acids, mucopolysaccharides, others|\n|Complex molecule degradation|Mucopolysaccharides, oligosaccharides, complex lipids|\n|Vitamin and cofactor metabolism|Vitamins, amino acids, organic acids|\n|Trace elements/metals metabolism|Trace elements/metals|\n|Neurotransmitter disorders|Biogenic amines, amino acids, organic acids|\n|Endocrine-metabolic disorders|Insulin/steroids|\n\n\n*The category of mitochondrial disorders has been simplified. In IEMbase (www.IEMBASE.org), mitochondrial disorders are classified into different categories: mitochondrial DNA disorders, nuclear-encoded mitochondrial genes, mitochondrial cofactors biosynthesis, mitochondrial DNA maintenance and replication, mitochondrial gene expression, and other mitochondrial disorders. \n\n# **Imaging Techniques, Radiomics, and Imaging Biomarkers** \n\nImaging is an extremely useful adjuvant method for diagnosis, supporting evidence to the clinical suspicion, or providing some clues about alternative diagnosis. Sometimes, imaging findings can be pathognomonic, but often they help narrow the differential diagnosis. It can be of invaluable help in guiding genetic or other tests toward a specific disease or group of diseases. This approach applies to fetal studies, newborns, toddlers, and older children. \n\nQuantitative radiological findings are rapidly moving from research to becoming more and more integrated into the clinical practice, moving radiology from classical and fundamentally descriptive interpretation toward scientific data-based analysis, which at the same time can provide better and easier integration with other quantitative biological omics data. Proper testing and standardization are mandatory in this setting before incorporating any image biomarker into clinical practice. \n\n## **Imaging Techniques** \n\nImaging techniques differ in the types of physical tools used and how they are applied. Each has its strengths and weaknesses. Most study structural tissue properties, but some can also be used to evaluate normal or abnormal tissue functioning. Contrast media can be used to enhance different tissue properties in many imaging techniques. Biomarkers can technically be developed from any imaging technique. \n\n## **_Plain Films_** \n\nPlain film uses X-ray to provide information on the density of the tissues in a single bidimensional image and was historically the first modality. However, it is the most used medical imaging due to its affordability and excellent initial approach to bone and lung abnormalities (Fig. 2.16A). In the context of rare diseases, it is an essential tool in evaluating patients with **skeletal dysplasia** . Extracting quantitative data from plain films is feasible, but that information is lower than that of other imaging options.\n\n<!-- p.55 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0056_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.16** A pictorial review of the different imaging modalities. (A) Chest X-ray: postsurgical X-ray of a neonate with truncus arteriosus. The heart’s widening and abnormal shape, together with basal left lung opacities. (B) Echocardiography: morphology and function synchronized with the ECG can be assessed accurately. (C) Brain CT: 10 years patient with Crouzon syndrome. Axial CT with bone algorithm and window shows detailed abnormalities of the bone. 3D volume renders reconstruction. (D) shows complex craniosynostosis, surgical changes, and ventriculoperitoneal shunting catheter. (E) Brain MRI: axial T2DRIVE sequence, same patient as in C and D. The right cochlea _(yellow arrow)_ and cochlear nerve _(blue arrow)_ are easily identified. (F and G) Brain MRI T2* perfusion: relative Cerebral Blood Flow map (F) and quantification (G). The red-colored nodule shows very high perfusion, consistent with relapse in a patient with ependymoma. Values can be measured; the _orange line_ is normal perfusion in the contralateral hemisphere, and the _blue line_ corresponds to the lesion. (H) PET-DOTA: same patient as in F and G. High uptake is consistent with high amino acid metabolism and thus relapse. (I) PET-DOTA fusion with the CT scan. \n\n## **_Ultrasound_** \n\nUltrasound uses high-frequency sound waves to create realtime images of soft tissues and solid organs that are not hidden behind bone or air. It is considered the safest biological imaging technique and is widely used in pediatrics. Besides acquiring 2D images or 3D volume, vascular flow can be assessed with Doppler studies (Fig. 2.16B), and tissue stiffness can be evaluated using shear wave techniques. \n\n## **_Computed Tomography_** \n\nCT can be visually processed in multiple planes and analyzed with many postprocessing techniques for 3D viewing, building 3D models, or printing. Because the acquisition is very fast, sedation is usually not required. CT provides excellent detail of the bone structure (Fig. 2.16C and D) and the lungs. Spectral CT can identify different atomic number (Z) components of the tissue and provide different maps according to its composition. The development of photon-counting detectors can also provide even higher spatial resolution (far below 1 mm) and better image quality with an improved contrast-to-noise ratio. Numeric quantification of the voxels is straightforward and widely used in radiological clinical practice. However, due to \n\nradioprotection concerns, CT use should be restricted, and the minimum possible radiation dose should be consistently applied to provide the required clinical information, following the ALARA principle (As Low As Reasonably Achievable). \n\n## **_Magnetic Resonance Imaging_** \n\nMagnetic resonance imaging (MRI) uses electromagnetic waves inside a strong magnetic field, usually between 1.5 and 3 T, to study different tissue properties and generates different sets of data (Fig. 2.16E), classically images (2D or 3D volumes) or tissue spectroscopy composition, but can also be used to assess microstructure properties, perfusion (Fig. 2.16F and G), connectivity, and function, among other options. Loosely speaking, each combination of electromagnetic waves combined with minor field strength variations are called sequences, and they provide different tissue information. H nuclei are studied in the clinical setting, but P31, C13, or Na23 can also be analyzed with dedicated equipment. \n\nClinical applications are enormous; MRI is widely used to study the brain and spine, soft tissues, heart, and musculoskeletal pathologies. Because many undiagnosed diseases may include inflammatory or infiltrative disorders, whole-body\n\n<!-- p.56 -->\n\nrapid short time inversion recovery (STIR) MRI has been useful in detecting abnormal marrow signals, soft tissue and bone or joint findings, and other abnormalities. A critical drawback of MRI is that it requires a considerable amount of acquisition time and usually complete stillness of the patient, so sedation or even general anesthesia in children is sometimes needed. \n\n## **_Nuclear Medicine_** \n\nNuclear medicine involves the application of small amounts of radioactive material combined with a carrier molecule (radiotracer) to examine organ function. Once it is administered, it follows the metabolic pathway that the bound molecule normally would, and radioactivity maps are taken and measured. This approach can sometimes also be used to deliver targeted radiation therapy. The most used imaging \n\nmodalities are scintigraphy, single photon emission (SPECT), and positron emission tomography (PET). There are many different radiopharmaceuticals available: metaiodo-benzyl-guanidine (mIBG) for neuroblastoma, fluorodeoxyglucose as a biomarker of glucose metabolism or amino acids in brain tumors, and dotatate (an analogue of somatostatin) for neuroendocrine tumors (Fig. 2.16H and I, Fig. 2.17). Drawbacks of these techniques are the use of radiation, long acquisition times, and low spatial resolution, which can be overcome by imaging fusion with CT or MRI. Clinical applications in pediatrics are extensive, the most common in nephro-urology, oncology, and inflammatory diseases. \n\n**Magnetic resonance spectroscopy** often with MRI is helpful in providing information in many mitochondrial diseases (Fig. 2.18). \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0057_07.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.17** Bronchial carcinoid in a 13-year-old female with incidentally discovered right lower lobe mass on a chest radiograph obtained for evaluation of cough and pneumonia. (A–C) Maximum-intensity projection coronal (A), fused coronal (B), and fused axial (C) images from a<sup>68</sup> Ga-Dotatate positron emission tomography (PET)/CT scan demonstrate marked tracer localization near the right hilum _(arrow)_ , with maximum standardized uptake value (SUVmax) = 47.6, consistent with a neuroendocrine tumor. This was resected and was confirmed to be a carcinoid tumor.  (From McElroy KM, Binkovitz LA, Trout AT, et al: Pediatric applications of dotatate: early diagnostic and therapeutic experience. _Pediatr Radiol_ . 2020;50:882–897 [Fig. 7, p. 888].)\n\n<!-- p.57 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0058_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.18** Bilateral putaminal nuclei in a 19-month-old patient with Leigh syndrome due to the 8993T>G variant in the mtDNA. An elevated inverted lactate is found at this echo time (135 ms) acquired on a 1.5 T Avanto scanner (Siemens Medical System, TE = 135, TR = 1.5 s, 16 16 matrix, interpolated to 32 32, 16 cm). (From Saneto RP, Friedman SD, Shaw DWW. Neuroimaging of mitochondrial disease. _Mitochondrion._ 2008;8:396-413.) \n\n## **Radiomics and Biomarker Development in Radiology** \n\nRadiomics focuses on pulling out quantitative data from medical images so this information can be data-mined to provide meaningful and valuable clinical information and data from other medical sources for precision medicine. There is one important advantage in comparison with the other omics, along with its noninvasiveness: it is not limited to the analysis of a specific tissue sample, but it can study the whole acquired anatomy. \n\nThe quantitative extraction approach of the imaging data provides a new framework for analyzing completely new tissue properties and identifying very subtle abnormalities or changes that might be very difficult to assess or quantify visually (Fig. 2.19). \n\nMost radiomics research has been first developed for oncological applications but is rapidly expanding to other areas. This approach, combined with machine learning and artificial intelligence, which can handle large data sets and analyze new features, proposes great expectations to enable more precise research analysis that could be transformed into better clinical practice and outcomes. This path, though, has many challenges, such as appropriate data sample size and process standardization. An important reference \n\nguideline can be found on the International Biomarker Standardisation Initiative’s (IBSI) website (https://ibsi. readthedocs.io/en/latest/index.html). In the context of rare diseases, this approach is even more difficult. The sample size is inherently scarcer than in other diseases, and, as in the clinical phenotype, there can be important variations among patients suffering from the same disease, so a multiinstitutional approach is desired to gather as much data as possible. Each institution has imaging equipment, probably from different vendors, so acquisition standardization should be mandatory for a correct analysis. The best current approach is using a phantom to calibrate the equipment acquisition combined with some statistical analysis correction approach for multiple comparisons such as ComBat. Also, standardization of the postprocessing pipeline is mandatory, as sometimes different pipelines or software versions have produced different results. There is the need to compare this information with standard data sets. Because recruiting controls is an option but can be very difficult, the tendency is to acquire large databases open to the scientific community. Some examples of brain MRI are the Developing Human Connectome Project initiative (https://www.developingconnectome.org/) for newborns and the Adolescent Brain Cognitive Development repository (ABCD) from the NIH (https://nda.nih.gov/abcd/) for older children.\n\n<!-- p.58 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0059_02.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 2.19** Morphometric quantitative brain MRI analysis. (A) Axial 3D T1 with segmentation and labeling with FreeSurfer (Laboratory for Computational Neuroimaging, Athinoula A. Martinos Center for Biomedical Imaging, Harvard Medical School). This approach allows the quantification of many different metrics of the brain tissue, such as cortical thickness maps (B, in mm) and surface representation (C). (D and E) Patient with mucopolysaccharidosis type III, at 3 and 4 years old, without evident visual changes. However, with this quantitative approach (F, in mm<sup>3</sup> ) we can clearly see a global loss of gray matter, specifically cortical volume. \n"});
window.__registerChapter({"num": 3, "slug": "003_The_Approach_to_Rare_Diseases_From_Local_to_Global", "title": "The Approach to Rare Diseases: From Local to Global", "authors": "David Adams, Gareth Baynam, Mariela Larrandaburu, Helen Malherbe, Carmencita D. Padilla, Francesc Palau", "part_num": "I", "part_title": "Introductory Chapters", "word_count": 9252, "pearls": ["Up to 80% of rare diseases are genetic, and most follow either Mendelian or mitochondrial monogenic inheritance, meaning that the underlying cause is a pathogenic variant in autosomal dominant, X-linked, or mitochondrial cases and biallelic pathogenic variants in autosomal recessive cases.", "This incentivization is the objective of orphan drug policies that provide dedicated resource support when a drug, gene therapy, or cell therapy is specifically intended for the treatment of a RD.", "Both the United States and Canada have ceded decisions about newborn screening (NBS) to individual states and provinces, respectively.", "EURORDIS also supports relevant public health and clinical actions, such as newborn screening, favoring the incorporation of genome sequencing to expand the early detection of more actionable genetic RDs.", "The Consortium on Newborn Screening in Africa (CONSA), launched in 2020, aims to standardize newborn hemoglobinopathy screening and early intervention for children with sickle cell disease in SSA.", "In addition, the policy has also provided for the inclusion of 40 rare diseases in its expanded newborn screening program starting in 2022.", "Enhancing diagnostic capacity, including expanding newborn screening, and increasing the number of genetic specialists and specialty care facilities will further improve diagnostic and management access.", "The Philippines established a central laboratory in 1997 for a nationwide newborn screening system."], "genes": [], "omims": [], "subtopics": ["The Approach to Rare Diseases From Local to Global", "An Overview of Rare Diseases", "Public Health", "Natural History: Diversity and Heterogeneity", "Comprehensive and Multidisciplinary Care", "Coordinated and Integrated Health and Social Care", "Genetics, Inheritance, and Risk of Recurrence", "Geographical Dispersion", "Treatment Opportunities and Therapy Development", "Global Health", "Rare Disease Diagnostics", "From Local to Global", "Canada and the United States", "Demographics and Epidemiology"], "figs": [{"img": "fig_p0064_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 3.1", "caption": "Rare disease prevalence per 100,000 across countries and jurisdictions. The size of the circles corresponds to the average prevalence threshold (number of cases per 100,000 people). Black and white circles correspond to jurisdictions in which the average prevalence in definitions of rare disease is lower or higher, respectively, than the average across all jurisdictions of 40 cases/100,000 people. Map lines delineate study areas and do not necessarily depict accepted national boundaries."}], "tables_meta": [{"label": "TABLE 3.1", "title": "Global and M f ultinational Coordinated Efforts for Rare Diseases"}, {"label": "TABLE 3.2", "title": "European a f nd Eastern Mediterranean Coordinated Efforts for Rare Dise f ases"}], "body_md": "\n<!-- p.62 -->\n\n# 3 The Approach to Rare Diseases From Local to Global \n\n**DAVID ADAMS, GARETH BAYNAM, MARIELA LARRANDABURU, HELEN MALHERBE, CARMENCITA D. PADILLA, FRANCESC PALAU** \n\n## **An Overview of Rare Diseases** \n\nRare diseases (RDs) are a global healthcare priority and a challenge for health professionals, scientific researchers, affected individuals, and their families. Despite highly diverse clinical findings, pathophysiologies, and prognoses, RDs share many common features. The majority (80%) result from genetic causes, individually affect a limited number of people, and are chronic and frequently disabling. Additionally, most RDs onset in childhood (up to 70%), result in high morbidity and mortality, and represent the largest proportion of cause-of-death in children in high-income countries. Therapeutic resources for RDs are generally limited. In the United States, the Rare Diseases Act of 2002 defines a rare disease as one that affects fewer than 200,000 individuals. The European Union establishes that a disorder or health condition can be labeled as a rare disease if the number of people affected is fewer than 5 per 10,000; prevalence rates that define a disorder as rare vary among other countries and world regions, with the global prevalence threshold averaging 4 per 10,000 individuals (Fig. 3.1). In 2022, Rare Diseases International (RDI), working with a global panel of experts and collaborating with the World Health Organization (WHO) International Classification of Diseases (ICD), developed an internationally endorsed _Operational Description of Rare Diseases:_ A rare disease is a medical condition with a specific pattern of clinical signs, symptoms, and findings that affects fewer than or equal to 1 in 2000 persons living in any WHOdefined region of the world. Rare diseases include but are not limited to rare genetic diseases. They can also include rare cancers, rare infectious diseases, rare poisonings, rare immune-related diseases, rare idiopathic diseases, and rare undetermined conditions. \n\nSome rare diseases are relatively common, such as cystic fibrosis or spinal muscular atrophy, but the majority are **ultra-rare** , affecting fewer than 1 in 50,000 individuals. This concept of rarity raises a social and health dilemma \n\ntermed the **rarity paradox** : each individual disease is infrequent but the collective group of _people living with a rare disease (PLWRD)_ is large. In aggregate, it is estimated that there are over 300 million people in the world affected by rare disorders. \n\nBroader social awareness of the challenges faced by PLWRD has progressively increased over time, largely due to the patient’s own advocacy and to the actions of rare disease organizations representing affected individuals and their families. Common and cross-cutting themes that have drawn attention include difficulty in obtaining an early and accurate diagnosis (i.e., the **diagnostic odyssey** , which lasts more than 5 years on average); uncertainty in genetic interpretations of gene variants of unknown significance (VUS); in RD with a specific genetic abnormality a paucity of specific therapies to correct the gene deficit including gene therapies; the lack of multidisciplinary, coordinated, and integrated intersectoral care (for example, among health, education, and social services); the shortage of specific interventions for mental health and well-being; the lack of information about rare diseases; the need for support at the time of diagnosis; personal and family-centered care; and the availability of research on the pathophysiology of and therapies for rare diseases. \n\n### **Public Health** \n\nLabeling an individual disorder or group of disorders (e.g., Friedreich ataxia or inherited cerebellar ataxias) as rare collectively elevates them to a public health priority. Scleroderma or Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, even with prevalence at the higher end of being categorized as rare diseases (at 4.2 and 2.8 per 10,000 individuals, respectively), are not necessarily considered sizeable individual challenges to public health. However, the collective impact of RDs (for example, 1 in 17 individuals in the Middle East and North African region lives with a rare disease) necessitates a public health-focused scientific and healthcare\n\n<!-- p.63 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0064_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 3.1** Rare disease prevalence per 100,000 across countries and jurisdictions. The size of the circles corresponds to the average prevalence threshold (number of cases per 100,000 people). _Black and white circles_ correspond to jurisdictions in which the average prevalence in definitions of rare disease is lower or higher, respectively, than the average across all jurisdictions of 40 cases/100,000 people. Map lines delineate study areas and do not necessarily depict accepted national boundaries. (From Richter T, Nestler-Parr S, Babela R, et al: Rare disease terminology and definitions—a systematic global review: Report of the ISPOR rare disease special interest group. Value Health 2015;18:906-914 [Fig. 1, p. 911].) \n\napproach. To focus public health approaches, reliable data on the prevalence, epidemiology, and individual and collective burden of RDs in the population must be established. \n\nindividual to another, which guides the clinical and preventive actions taken by health services. In the case of Mendelian diseases, the profiles may vary within the same family. \n\n### **Natural History: Diversity and Heterogeneity** \n\nNatural history refers to the causes of the disease _(etiology)_ , the age of onset and temporal development of the disease _(chronobiology)_ , the clinical expression _(semiology and pathophysiology)_ , and the degree of severity _(seriousness and prognosis)_ . In the case of RDs, natural histories are diverse. The nature of the pathological processes varies from diseases that affect a single organ system to multisystem diseases; in the case of retinitis pigmentosa and the group of retinal dystrophies, the affected organ is the eye and individuals suffer from a sensory disorder. In muscular dystrophies, skeletal muscle is the affected structure, and individuals suffer from locomotor dysfunction. On the other hand, in the case of metabolic conditions such as Gaucher disease, the disorder affects different organ systems including the hematopoietic, nervous, and skeletal systems. Similarly, systemic diseases like lupus erythematosus or scleroderma involve several organs. The heterogeneity of these diseases becomes apparent in their diverse natural history profiles, which vary from one disease to another and from one \n\n### **Comprehensive and Multidisciplinary Care** \n\nA rare disease is often chronic and debilitating. In many cases, affected individuals require care that goes beyond that offered by a single specialized healthcare provider. This care must be considered in the context of global management guidelines, involving pediatric or family medicine primary care providers, the medical specialty or specialties that understand specialty-specific clinical problems, nursing, physiotherapy, social services, and psychological support. For many affected individuals, especially those with multiple impacted organs or systems, comprehensive care requires the reconciliation of multiple perspectives, incorporating different areas of expertise into a single coordination framework of multidisciplinary care. \n\n### **Coordinated and Integrated Health and Social Care** \n\nMany rare diseases involve a high degree of dependence upon the healthcare system and entail significant social,\n\n<!-- p.64 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0065_01.jpg)\n\n\nhealth, and economic burdens. Although this challenge is not unique to rare diseases, there are certain aspects of rare diseases that differentiate them, largely due to the notion that the first symptoms of many of these diseases occur in childhood or adolescence, resulting in a lifelong personal, family, and social burden. Consequently, PLWRD have specific healthcare, social, educational, and occupational needs over a long period, which requires constant, ongoing provision of planned, coordinated long-term action and management. \n\n### **Genetics, Inheritance, and Risk of Recurrence** \n\nUp to 80% of rare diseases are genetic, and most follow either Mendelian or mitochondrial monogenic inheritance, meaning that the underlying cause is a pathogenic variant in autosomal dominant, X-linked, or mitochondrial cases and biallelic pathogenic variants in autosomal recessive cases. Increasing knowledge of genetic disease, including genetic variability, gene pathways, and relationships between genotype and phenotype, adds to the understanding of the epidemiology of disease variants in specific populations. The risk of recurrence of single-gene, genomic (i.e., microdeletion and microduplication), and chromosomal disorders makes genetic counseling an essential component of rare disease healthcare for both affected individuals and their families. \n\n### **Geographical Dispersion** \n\nThe random distribution of PLWRD across broad geographic regions is an intrinsic component of rare diseases and means that new cases can occur anywhere, whether in an urban or rural environment, without being concentrated in any specific area. Exceptions to this rule occur in circumstances where the isolation of a population has caused a bottleneck and **founder effect** of disease-causing pathogenic variants, with a consequent increase in the carrier rate in that population, or the presence of a population cluster due to environmental problems (e.g., Lyme disease, heavy metals). This geographical dispersion makes it difficult to implement specific actions aimed at these diseases, especially in resourcelimited environments. \n\n### **Treatment Opportunities and Therapy Development** \n\nTherapeutic options for RDs are few and many are ineffective. Research and development of new therapies has historically required and continues to require incentivization. This incentivization is the objective of orphan drug policies that provide dedicated resource support when a drug, gene therapy, or cell therapy is specifically intended for the treatment of a RD. Obtaining sufficient evidence on the safety and efficacy of these therapies in PLWRD is difficult, especially when many affected individuals are children. The design of clinical trials is further challenged by the limited availability and interest of clinical researchers, \n\nfinancing capacity, and the recruitment of patients with the correct diagnosis who meet the inclusion criteria. \n\n### **Global Health** \n\nWhile low-and middle-income countries have traditionally focused most of their public health efforts on infectious diseases and poor nutrition, rare disease care is increasingly identified as a priority for all countries and populations, regardless of national economic status. Incorporating rare disease care into universal healthcare policies and aligning local interventions with global actions requires significant collaboration including the improvement of national and regional health systems and social structures. The International Rare Diseases Research Consortium (IRDiRC) was established in 2011 and has identified three major goals to achieve by 2027: (i) to provide all individuals with a suspected RD a diagnosis within 1 year; (ii) to approve a thousand new therapies for RDs; and (iii) to develop new methodologies for assessing the impact of RD diagnoses and therapies. In December 2021, the General Assembly of the United Nations (UN) Member States adopted the resolution proposed by EURORDIS-Rare Diseases Europe, Rare Disease International (RDI), and the Committee on Non-Governmental Organizations for RDs, which aims to promote greater integration of RDs in the Sustainable Development Goals of the 2030 Agenda. These global actions are important milestones in achieving greater awareness and recognition of RDs. Along with global actions, additional disease delineation and coding efforts, such as the ORPHAcode, help facilitate proper diagnoses and treatments. \n\n## **Rare Disease Diagnostics** \n\nAchieving a high-confidence diagnosis for an individual with a rare disease is often delayed relative to symptom onset, with an average delay of 6 to 8 years. The causes of diagnostic delays for PLWRD are multifactorial. \n\nMedical training prioritizes efficient recognition of disorders that are common and treatable over diseases that may not be seen in the lifetime of a provider, exemplifying the common saying, adapted from an aphorism coined by Dr. Theodore Woodward, “When you hear hoofbeats, think horses, not zebras.” The diagnosis of a RD may require esoteric testing that is not widely available. Expertise related to a RD may be rare; many families report that they are more expert in their disorder than most or all their medical providers. In the age of clinical genomic medicine, the number of newly defined diseases has increased dramatically, often outpacing the ability of any one individual clinician to stay updated on the latest advances. In addition, diagnosing diseases by genomic testing rather than by phenotype recognition has expanded the phenotypic scope for many disorders, broadening the understanding of how those disorders typically present and are diagnosed. Further inequalities arise from economic, geographic, and other social factors.\n\n<!-- p.65 -->\n\nAll these factors pose significant barriers to rapid, definitive RD diagnosis. \n\nPatients and families may spend many years in an undiagnosed state, either because of diagnostic delay for a known rare disorder or the presence of a yet-to-be-described medical condition. This undiagnosed state presents affected individuals with additional challenges. Well-meaning providers may offer a protracted set of testing options, creating a seemingly unending cycle of potentially expensive, invasive, and inconclusive interventions. Similarly, symptomatic management plans may not be directed at underlying pathophysiology and may introduce iatrogenic symptoms, confounding the disease phenotype. Families and individuals frequently report uncomfortable encounters with both medical providers and other social contacts when they are unable to name a reason for their or their child’s medical problems. Not having a diagnosis may impede the characterization of an accurate prognosis or identification of a best treatment. Finally, both rare and undiagnosed diseases leave many affected individuals feeling isolated, both in terms of their disease-related experience and the curtailment of social contacts by disease management obligations, reducing their quality of life. \n\n## **Mental Health and Well-Being, Stigma, and Considerations in Indigenous and Other Culturally and Linguistically Diverse Populations** \n\nPeople living with a RD have unique risk factors and vulnerabilities that compound to adversely affect mental health and well-being. At an individual level, poor mental health is a comorbidity specifically associated with many RDs; at a population level, increased psychological distress is associated with the rare disease journey across all stages of life. PLWRD and their families additionally are exposed to social inequalities and discrimination at disproportionate rates. \n\nWhile RDs are medically heterogeneous, their rarity results in numerous common stressors, including long diagnostic journeys, limited access to and availability of treatments, uncoordinated care pathways, as well as the challenges of living with disabilities, limited work opportunities, unemployment and underemployment, isolation, and social stigma. Most rare diseases onset in childhood and these stressors are often interdependent and accumulate over the lifespan. Up to 85% of PLWRD indicate that their condition impacts multiple aspects of their everyday life and their physical and mental health; PLWRD report three times higher levels of unhappiness and depression than the general population. \n\nAdditionally, the lack of focus on the mental health and well-being of PLWRD contributes to a hidden mental health burden that is often overshadowed by complex physical health challenges. This further marginalizes and excludes people with RD across all facets of their life, including health, education, and community integration. \n\nThe RD community has identified as an absolute priority the need to look beyond the physical symptoms of a RD and take concrete action to address psychological impact. \n\n**Stigma** , as seminally defined in the work of 20th century sociologist Erving Goffman, is a discrediting attribute that distinguishes an individual from others by reducing them “from a whole and usual person to a tainted, discounted one.” Stigma is a critical health and social barrier that is identified as an explicit challenge by up to 1 in 3 PLWRD, equating to more than 100 million worldwide, or approximately 3 times the global population living with HIV. People with RDs experience _structurally enacted stigma_ in the forms of healthcare stigma, education or workplace stigma, and an overall lack of accessibility. They also face _interpersonally enacted_ stigma, including insufficient social support, a lack of understanding from others, and capitalist norms of productivity and self-sufficiency. PLWRD experience additional stigma related to shame and the pressure to pass as _able-bodied_ . Stigma generates inequality, marginalization, discrimination, and exclusion that impair access to health, social, and community services. Stigma frequently prevents the start of or progress through diagnostic, therapeutic, and care odysseys. Stigma’s physical, mental, and social consequences are likely a key contributor to the lower quality of life reported by PLWRD compared to those with more common diseases. Until destigmatization is globally prioritized and comprehensively reviewed through an international lens, and the pathways for implementing culturally safe and responsive mechanisms are identified and acted upon, the global goals for diagnosis, care, and social inclusion cannot be met. \n\nIndigenous and other culturally and linguistically diverse populations frequently have challenged access to RD diagnosis and care services due to decreased referral rates, referral bias, fewer and more geographically remote care locations, higher care costs, lower availability of resources and care personnel, and lack of cultural appropriateness. This cultural appropriateness includes education materials that resonate with Indigenous narratives and that incorporate Indigenous languages, meeting places and clinical processes that accommodate familial and community participation, consent processes adaptable to family structures, involvement of culturally appropriate support and liaison persons for addressing racism and discrimination, cultural competency training among RD healthcare staff, and improved capacity to care for rare diseases in primary care settings. \n\n## **From Local to Global** \n\nWhile RDs are not included as a specific health topic on the WHO agenda, there is an evolving WHO Global Network for Rare Diseases; this effort and other global initiatives for RDs are presented in Table 3.1. RDI advocates for global recognition of RDs as a public health concern within the WHO Universal Health Coverage agenda. The UN, led by the Non-Governmental Organization (NGO) Committee\n\n<!-- p.66 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 3.1** Global and M f ultinational Coordinated Efforts for Rare Diseases\n\n|Action|Concept|URL|\n|---|---|---|\n|Rare Diseases<br>International<br>(RDI)|RDI promotes international initiatives such as the Global<br>Network for Rare Disease, supporting the rare disease<br>community in developing a person-centered global network<br>of care and expertise for all people living with a rare disease,<br>and the adoption of 12 main recommendations to be<br>addressed at international level as a matter of priority.|https://www.rarediseasesinternational.<br>org/collaborative-global-network/.<br>https://www.<br>rarediseasesinternational.org/joint-<br>declaration/<br>https://www.rarediseasesinternational.<br>org/description-for-rd/|\n|The United Nations|The NGO Committee for Rare Diseases is a substantive<br>committee established under the umbrella of the Conference<br>of NGOs (nongovernmental organizations) in Consultative<br>Relationship with the United Nations (CoNGO). The goal is to<br>promote rare diseases as a priority in global health, research,<br>and social and medical care as part of the UN 2030 Agenda:<br>the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs).|http://download2.eurordis.org.<br>s3.amazonaws.com/<br>ngocommittee/ngocommittee_<br>report.pdf|\n|World Health<br>Organization,<br>International<br>Classification of<br>Diseases—ICD11|ICD-11 includes some 5,500 rare diseases and their synonyms.<br>The rare diseases are regularly updated, in close collaboration<br>and conceptual identity with Orphanet. The work is also<br>linked to the WHO Collaborative Global Network for Rare<br>Diseases.|https://www.who.int/standards/<br>classifications/frequently-asked-<br>questions/rare-diseases<br>https://www.who.int/standards/<br>classifications/classification-of-<br>diseases|\n|The International<br>Rare Diseases<br>Research<br>Consortium|An international action on research to enable all people living<br>with a rare disease to receive accurate diagnosis, care, and<br>therapy available within 1 year of receiving medical attention.|https://irdirc.org/|\n|Undiagnosed<br>Diseases<br>Network<br>International<br>(UDNI)|An international network focused on addressing undiagnosed<br>rare diseases as conditions that elude diagnosis.|https://www.udninternational.org/|\n\n\nfor Rare Diseases, RDI, and EURORDIS-Rare Diseases Europe, has placed a statement on RDs on its Sustainable Development Goals 2030 Agenda; the UN Resolution of Addressing the Challenges of Persons and Families with Rare Diseases was unanimously adopted by all UN Member States. As a global patient movement, RDI has adopted 12 recommendations as a matter of priority, which include issues related to RD visibility, patient empowerment, universal access to healthcare, research, networking of patient registries, and cooperation in policy formulation at the international level. However, health, social, and economic structures differ across various countries and geographic regions around the world; addressing these specific regional barriers and moving from fragmented local to unified global care continues to be a challenge. \n\n### **Canada and the United States** \n\nCanada and the United States are large, high-income countries known for extensive investment in medical research and healthcare. Canada is the second largest country, covering approximately 10 million square kilometers. Most of the population is concentrated in a few areas in the \n\nsouthern half of the country. The United States is the fourth largest country, covering 9.8 million square kilometers. Both countries have diverse populations, socioeconomic settings, and healthcare facility concentrations. \n\n### **_Demographics and Epidemiology_** \n\nThe US 2023 population is estimated at 340 million, having grown from 331 million in 2020 (population growth rate 0.7%). The birth rate is 12.21 births/1,000 (149th in the world). The population is 60% White, 12% Black or African American, 10% “mixed,” and 6% Asian. Hispanic populations are inconsistently defined, but comprise approximately 18% of the population. Indigenous populations make up less than 2%. The median age is 38 years. The mean maternal age at first birth is 27 years. Maternal mortality is 32.9 deaths/100,000 live births. The infant mortality rate is 5.6 deaths/1,000 live births. Land use is 45% agricultural. Health expenditure is 18.8% of GDP with 2.6 physicians/1,000 population. \n\nCanada’s population is approximately 39 million. Self-described ethnicities include approximately 80% Old Canadian and/or European. Other populations include First Nations (1.7%) and Indian (3.7%). The population growth rate is 0.7%. The country has two official languages,\n\n<!-- p.67 -->\n\nEnglish and French. The median age is 41.8 years. The mean maternal age at first birth is 29 years. Maternal mortality is 11/100,000 live births, and infant mortality is 4.4 deaths/1,000 live births. Health expenditure is 12.9% of GDP with 2.4 physicians/1,000 population. \n\nBoth countries have large, relatively stable economies. Canada’s real GDP is 1.8 trillion; the US’s real GDP is 21 trillion. \n\n### **_The Rare Disease Landscape in Canada and the United States_** \n\nFor both Canada and the United States, the past two decades have seen a rapid expansion in RD advocacy groups. Groups range in size, resources, and focus. In addition to disease-specific groups, broader networks have emerged. Broad networks may encompass related diseases or provide resources of general utility to many disease-specific groups. Example initiatives undertaken by advocacy groups include the generation of formal registries, fundraising and research support, direct support to families, political advocacy, testing referrals, and the provision of institutional review board functionality for formal studies. \n\nThe United States passed the **Orphan Drug Act** in 1983, creating incentives for RD pharmacologic therapy development. The **Rare Disease Act of 2002** established a definition of rarity (<200,000 cases in the United States), established a RD office at the National Institutes of Health, and provided funding for a research network for selected rare diseases (Rare Diseases Clinical Research Network or RDCRN). Examples include the Genetic Alliance (https://geneticalliance.org/), the Rare Epilepsy Network (https://www.rareepilepsynetwork.org/), the National Organization for Rare Diseases (https://rarediseases.org/), the Undiagnosed Diseases Network (https://undiagnosed.hms.harvard.edu/), and Global Genes (https://global genes.org/). These groups foster communication and infrastructure development among the RD community. Many have international components and see their scope as larger than just the US population. \n\nCanada published a National Strategy for Rare Diseases in March of 2022. It includes 1.5 billion dollars of funding over three years and goals focused on patient outcomes and investment in innovation. Exemplar RD organizations in Canada include the Canadian Organization for Rare Disorder (CORD, https://www.raredisorders.ca/) and Care for Rare (https://www.care4rare.ca/). \n\n### **_Healthcare Coverage, Infrastructure, and Services for Rare Diseases_** \n\nHealthcare provision in the United States consists of a diverse collection of institutions, funders, and settings. Most day-to-day medical care is provided by private or nonprofit healthcare organizations that own systems of regional hospitals, laboratories, and clinics. These organizations may be associated with academic tertiary healthcare centers. Many large cities have public hospitals with some capacity to treat under-and uninsured individuals. Payment for services is either by private insurance, government \n\nsubsidy, or self-payment. Government-provided healthcare funding is provided to certain segments of the population, such as low-income or elderly individuals. Available programs and funding levels vary between US states and are subject to political forces. \n\nIn Canada, 70% of healthcare is provided by 10 provincial governments, providing a healthcare coverage safety net. \n\nBoth Canada and the United States have broad primary prevention initiatives through routine primary medical care. Examples include hazardous product regulation, safety campaigns, and childhood immunizations. Similarly, secondary prevention initiatives include pregnancy screening, childhood developmental screening, cancer screening, and policy infrastructure for workplace accommodations. Cultural, political, resource-related, socioeconomic, and other factors prevent universal deployment and uptake. An example in the United States is vaccine hesitancy, a term used to describe the refusal of available vaccinations for a variety of reasons. \n\n### **_Newborn Screening for Rare Diseases_** \n\nBoth the United States and Canada have ceded decisions about newborn screening (NBS) to individual states and provinces, respectively. Both countries have a complex process for adding new tests, with the “Recommended Uniform Screening Panel” in the United States serving as an example (https://www.hrsa.gov/advisory-committees/heritabledisorders/rusp). Regional population parameters, politics, and resource differences have resulted in a patchwork of offerings among locations. In general, both countries universally offer a core set of testing including metabolic disorders detected by mass spectrometry. Significant differences exist for other conditions—for example, lysosomal storage diseases. Both countries have established referral networks for follow-up of positive NBS results. Several pilot studies and a rapidly expanding fund of medical knowledge have explored the feasibility of genome-wide sequencing as an NBS methodology; however, this approach has not yet been implemented as standard practice. \n\n### **_Challenges_** \n\nDespite extensive investment in research and healthcare, barriers to RD diagnosis and treatment in World Bank high-income countries such as Canada and the United States are similar in many ways to barriers in other lowerincome countries. General health disparities related to income, race, socioeconomic status, and other factors are amplified for PLWRD. Necessary expertise is often still rare and concentrated in large urban environments. \n\nIn the United States, the combination of private and public healthcare reimbursement systems creates inequities in which individuals with financial and educational resources face fewer barriers navigating the healthcare system. One in 10 individuals in the United States is uninsured. In addition to insurance coverage status, health disparities in the United States are associated with race, education, education access, and other social determinants. In Canada, there\n\n<!-- p.68 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0069_01.jpg)\n\n\nare substantial challenges related to population aging, access to medical care from remote locations, cultural and linguistic diversity, and navigation of the unique rights of Canada’s Indigenous populations. \n\nBoth the United States and Canada have extensive healthcare delivery expertise and resources. Despite this expertise, both continue to face challenging problems related to equitable access and delivery of these resources. \n\n### **Latin America and the Caribbean** \n\nLatin America and the Caribbean are highly diverse, not just with respect to population genetics, but also geographically, demographically, ethnically, and socioculturally. This heterogeneity explains in part the numerous barriers PLWRD there face with respect to diagnosis, treatment, follow-up, access to medical care, social support, and education. \n\n### **_Demographics and Epidemiology_** \n\nIn 2022, the maternal mortality rate in Latin America and the Caribbean was 68/100,000 live births, and the infant mortality was 15.5/1,000 live births. Of the total number of PLWRD worldwide, less than 15% are Latin Americans. Among these, 13 million live in Brazil; between 7.5 and 10 million in Mexico; approximately 3 million in Colombia; a similar figure in Argentina; between 1 and 1.5 million in Chile, Peru, Bolivia, Paraguay, and Venezuela; and up to 175,000 in Uruguay. Thus, five countries comprise twothirds of those affected by rare diseases in this region (Brazil, Mexico, Colombia, Peru, and Argentina). \n\nThere is no consensus definition for rarity in Latin America. Colombia defines a RD as affecting fewer than 1 in every 5,000 people, while Argentina, Chile, Mexico, Panama, and Uruguay adopt the European Union definition of fewer than 5 in every 10,000 people. Brazil follows the WHO definition of 1 in 1,500 (65 in 100,000). Bolivia, Ecuador, Paraguay, Peru, and Venezuela lack a specific definition. These differences impact the number of identified affected individuals and create challenges in estimates, policies, and resources for research. \n\n### **_Health Coverage_** \n\nHealthcare systems in Latin America are fragmented, with variations in rights, coverage, and organization among population groups. There are public services for the poor, social security for formal employees (sometimes including families), and private services. Inefficient coordination between these sectors hinders universal health coverage. This fragmentation is also reflected in care for RDs, where some countries with specific laws, such as Colombia, Brazil, Argentina, and Chile, have genetic and genomic diagnoses and treatments available for some rare conditions. \n\n### **_Genetic Services_** \n\nOf the 15 countries in Latin America and the Caribbean, only 8 have medical genetics services. The current workforce in Latin America is approximately 1,100 consulting \n\nclinical geneticists; only one country (Nicaragua) offers a master’s degree in genetic counseling. The British Royal College of Physicians estimates that 0.75 full-time equivalent consultants are needed for every 250,000 inhabitants. In this sense, most Latin American countries, except for Peru and Uruguay, have an insufficient number of these specialists to care for their populations. \n\n### **_Capacity in Medical Genetics and Genomics_** \n\nEight countries offer some form of professional training in medical genetics and genomics within their own countries, and 6 offer a residency program in medical genetics. The role of genetic counselor is present only in Nicaragua. \n\n### **_Newborn Screening for Rare Diseases_** \n\nTandem mass spectrometry technology is implemented at national level in only 2 countries; however, testing programs are expanding. Congenital hypothyroidism is the most frequently tested pathology, followed by phenylketonuria, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, cystic fibrosis, and galactosemia. \n\n### **_Local Legislation and International Regulatory Framework_** \n\nOnly 6 Latin American countries have specific laws for RDs (Colombia, 2010; Peru and Argentina, 2011; Mexico, 2012; Brazil, 2014; Chile, 2015). These laws concern the rights to diagnosis, treatment, monitoring, and provision of medical care. However, many of these laws face numerous barriers that prevent their broad enforcement, development, and practical implementation. In addition, there are several UN and WHO resolutions or documents that, while lacking an enforcement mechanism, help leverage and generate funds that allow actions to be prioritized within the member states. \n\n### **_Collaborative Agreements and Institutions_** \n\nIn 2013, ALIBER (the Spanish-language acronym for the Ibero-American Rare Diseases Alliance) was established, formalizing partnerships and coordination among RD institutions in Spain, Portugal, 11 Latin American countries, and 4 additional European countries. In 2019, a collaborative proposal from Spain, Brazil, and Qatar was presented to the UN, leading to the incorporation of “Leave No One Behind Living with a Rare Disease” in the UN Sustainable Development Goals 2030 Agenda. In 2021, a historic resolution was unanimously adopted by all 197 member states, addressing “Challenges faced by People Living with Rare Diseases and Their Families.” \n\nAn ALIBER study named ENSERioLATAM is designed to identify and address various aspects of RDs in Latin America. While there is some heterogeneity in data collection among different regions, ENSERioLATAM has provided a valuable baseline to understand the healthcare needs of PLWRD in Latin America. Various scientific societies have also been instrumental in raising awareness and offering specialized training in RDs in Latin America. Notably the Latin American Human Genetics Network\n\n<!-- p.69 -->\n\n(RELAGH) actively promotes education in human and medical genetics and genomics. Many of these institutions work in coordination with the larger RD advocacy movement. \n\n### **Europe** \n\n#### **_Demographics and Epidemiology_** \n\nThe European region includes the countries of Western and Central Europe, the Russian Federation, the Central Asian republics, the Caucasus republics, Turkey, Lebanon, and Israel, with approximately 750 million inhabitants. Most of these countries have high or upper-middle income and have a public healthcare model, with a national health system that offers care services to the entire population, although each country has specific differences in administration; in some countries private services may also be relevant. Healthcare coverage includes genetic diagnosis and a genomebased approach to define the cause of a RD, but coverage is variable. Most countries recognize medical genetics and clinical laboratory genetics as a health specialty and formal \n\ntraining is offered as a university master’s degree for genetic counseling, with a European Board of Medical Genetics supporting educational development and implementation. \n\n### **_The Rare Disease Landscape in Europe_** \n\nThe European Union (EU) of 27 member states has developed policies and actions on RDs for over 25 years. During this time, the EU and other countries of the region have created an ecosystem for RDs that has favored the bidirectional participation of different stakeholders, from the European Union to the member states and vice versa, generating national and regional plans and strategies. The EU has advocated for orphan drug designation by the Committee of Orphan Medicinal Products (COMP) of the European Medicines Agency (EMA), research and innovation programs in the field of RDs, and the participation of various research groups and the pharmaceutical and biotechnological industries in an interstate context. The participation of affected individuals and their organizations has been fundamental, leading to a high degree of empowerment (Table 3.2). \n\n\n#### **TABLE 3.2** European a f nd Eastern Mediterranean Coordinated Efforts for Rare Dise f ases\n\n|Action|Concept|URL|\n|---|---|---|\n|Committee for<br>Orphan<br>Medicinal<br>Products<br>(COMP)|Established in 2000, COMP is the European Medicines Agency<br>committee responsible for recommending orphan designation of<br>medicines for rare diseases.|https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/<br>committees/committee-orphan-<br>medicinal-products-comp<br>https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/<br>human-regulatory/overview/<br>orphan-designation-overview|\n|European Union<br>(EU) National<br>Plans &<br>Strategies|A European Council recommendation to EU member states to<br>implement strategic plans for rare diseases to ensure that<br>patients have access to high-quality care, including diagnostics,<br>treatments, rehabilitation for those living with a rare disease,<br>and, if possible, effective orphan drugs.|https://health.ec.europa.eu/latest-<br>updates/national-plans-or-<br>strategies-rare-diseases-page-<br>updated-1970-01-01_en|\n|EURORDIS|Rare Diseases Europe is a nonprofit alliance of over 1,000 rare<br>disease patient organizations from 74 countries that work together<br>to improve the lives of people living with a rare disease, strengthen<br>the patient voice, and shape research, policies, and patient<br>services.|https://www.eurordis.org/|\n|European<br>Reference<br>Networks<br>(ERNs)|Virtual networks coordinating healthcare providers across Europe,<br>aimed to facilitate discussion on complex or rare diseases<br>requiring highly specialized treatment and concentrated<br>knowledge and resources.|https://health.ec.europa.eu/<br>european-reference-networks/<br>overview_en|\n|MENA<br>Organization for<br>Rare Diseases|MENA is the organization for rare diseases established to serve<br>and support people with rare diseases in the region of the<br>Middle East and North Africa (MENA).|https://www.menararediseases.<br>org|\n|Orphanet|Orphanet serves as a centralized database for resources and<br>knowledge on rare diseases so as to improve the diagnosis,<br>care, and treatment of patients with rare diseases. Orphanet also<br>maintains the Orphanet rare disease nomenclature (ORPHAcode).<br>Currently, over 6,000 disorders are associated with an<br>ORPHAcode.|https://www.orpha.net|\n|Rare 2030|The Rare 2030 Foresight Study, initiated by the European Parliament<br>and co-funded by the European Commission Pilot Project and<br>Preparatory Actions Programme, provided recommendations and<br>goals for rare disease-focused initiatives.|https://www.rare2030.eu/;http://<br>download2.eurordis.org/<br>rare2030/Rare2030_<br>recommendations.pdf|\n\n<!-- p.70 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0071_01.jpg)\n\n\nDuring this period there have been several significant milestones. In 1999, the Orphan Medicinal Product Regulation was approved. Between 2008 and 2011 the EU established a global community strategy to support member states in the diagnosis, treatment, and care of the 36 million EU citizens living with RDs, supported by national strategies (Table 3.2). The purpose was to improve the recognition and visibility of RDs, the promotion of research on these diseases, and the establishment of links between expert centers and professionals in different countries through the creation of European Reference Networks (ERNs) for 23 groups of rare and complex diseases and a network on pediatric transplantation. These networks are key in sharing knowledge and experience, and when necessary, identifying where patients should go when this experience cannot be made available to them locally. Directive 2011/24/EU, relating to the application of patient rights in cross-border healthcare, was approved in 2011, being of great importance for PLWRD while facilitating access to medical care for EU citizens and promoting cooperation between EU member states in the field of health. \n\nThe European Commission and its member states, with the participation of different stakeholders in rare diseases, has funded health projects such as Orphanet, a centralized database and information portal for RDs and orphan drugs. Orphanet includes detailed information on over 6,000 diseases, as well as services such as an encyclopedia for professionals in 9 languages and the national Orphanet websites of 31 countries in Europe and countries in other WHO regions and continents. \n\nEURORDIS-Rare Diseases Europe is the European Alliance for Rare Diseases, a nonprofit alliance of over 1,000 rare disease patient organizations from 74 countries. As a lay patient organization, EURORDIS advocates from the perspective of PLWRD. Selected initiatives include the European Conference on Rare Diseases and Orphan Products (ECRD), a globally recognized patient-led RD policy event, as well as the Rare 2030 Foresight Study that seeks to define what future is wanted for PLWRD in Europe. The Rare 2030 Foresight Study has proposed 8 recommendations to be implemented by 2030: long-term integrated European and individual national strategic plans; earlier, faster, more accurate diagnosis; access to high-quality healthcare; integrated and person-centered care; partnership with patients; innovative and needs-led research and development; optimizing data for patient and societal benefit; and available, accessible and affordable treatments. \n\nEURORDIS also supports relevant public health and clinical actions, such as newborn screening, favoring the incorporation of genome sequencing to expand the early detection of more actionable genetic RDs. \n\n### **_Newborn Screening for Rare Diseases_** \n\nCurrently, NBS programs have different implementations depending on the country. NBS for metabolic, endocrine, hematologic, immunologic, and neurologic disorders is implemented in most European countries, though with large differences between countries and at a regional level within countries. \n\n### **Middle East and North African Countries** \n\n#### **_Demographics and Epidemiology_** \n\nThe demic complexity of the Mediterranean Basin, an important intersection between three continents where different people and cultures have met over time, has shaped the genetic variability of Mediterranean populations. Migration, together with cultural exchanges and environmental factors, gave rise to present-day population genomic patterns. The WHO Eastern Mediterranean region comprises 21 member states and the Palestinian Authority, with a population of nearly 745 million people. With one affected person for every 17 individuals, it is estimated that there are approximately 43 million PLWRD in the region. \n\n### **_The Rare Disease Landscape in the Middle East and North African (MENA) Countries_** \n\nIn the MENA countries, RDs collectively impose a significant burden on healthcare systems, especially in low and medium-income or developing countries, where the lack of specialized care centers and limited access to genomic services contribute to longer diagnostic odysseys, resulting in missed opportunities for personalized management plans. Establishing clinical genomics and genetic counseling departments and facilities within tertiary reference hospitals is a major issue still to be addressed. \n\nThe MENA region has one of the highest documented prevalence rates of some genetic diseases in the world. Many factors may contribute to the recurrence of monogenic pathogenic trait expression, including founder genetic variants that arise in a single ancestral individual and diffuse through generations in isolated communities. Some populations within these countries experience relatively higher rates of consanguinity and endogamy that favor an increased prevalence of recessive genetic disorders. \n\nThe structure and focus of healthcare delivery within MENA countries has changed over the last decade, with improved management and treatment of common diseases leading to increased awareness of genetic RDs and identification of RDs as a public health priority. However, common diseases with a more direct and greater population burden often remain prioritized. Economic variability among different countries has also curtailed the development of a unified approach to RDs within this region: in the Arabian Peninsula, efforts to improve the diagnostic capacity for genetic diseases has increased substantially, while efforts in the countries of North Africa have not been as robust. While there are only a limited number of broad associations dedicated to RDs in the region, the MENA Organization for Rare Diseases is dedicated to advancing research, raising awareness, and providing support for individuals with RDs by organizing annual meetings in the region. \n\n### **Sub-Saharan Africa** \n\nThe Sub-Saharan Africa (SSA) region covers 23.9 million square kilometers and is divided into four sub-regions,\n\n<!-- p.71 -->\n\nWest, East, Central, and Southern Africa. SSA includes 32 countries and is home to numerous ethnic groups and over 2,000 languages. \n\n### **_Demographics and Epidemiology_** \n\nThe population of SSA is 1.21 billion and will double by 2050, when it is expected to account for 50% of the global population. The combined life expectancy at birth for the region is 59.7 years; age distribution skews younger, with only 3% of the population aged over 65 years. The regional fertility rate is 4.6 births per female. Rapid population growth in SSA has led many people to move from rural to urban settings, and the rapidity of this demographic change has resulted in specific challenges to health and well-being. \n\nThe region has been severely affected by the HIV/AIDS epidemic, with an adult HIV prevalence rate of 9%, compared to an overall worldwide adult HIV prevalence rate of 1.2%. An estimated 43% of global newborn deaths occur in the region, and a child born in SSA is 20 times more likely to die under 5 years of age than a child born in a highincome country. As of 2021, the regional under-5 mortality rate is 73 per 1,000 live births; infant mortality rate is 50 per 1,000 live births. \n\n### **_The Rare Diseases Landscape in SSA_** \n\nApproximately 30% of all global congenital anomalies, including many RDs, occur in SSA; 38% of all global deaths due to congenital anomalies in children under the age of 5 years occur in SSA. Causes for this degree of disease-related morbidity and mortality are multifactorial and include poverty, limited access to adequate nutrition, inadequate healthcare infrastructure, insufficient antenatal care combined with a high fertility rate and an increasing proportion of mothers of advanced maternal age, relatively higher levels of consanguinity, and environmental exposures. Up to 75% of the approximately 400,000 individuals with sickle cell disease born annually across the globe are born in SSA; this condition, along with other public health needs including the higher prevalence of adult HIV, has created a significant public health disease burden that leaves these and other more readily diagnosed and treated communicable diseases more prioritized relative to RDs. Compounding this resource allocation challenge is the oftentimes inadequate diagnostic capability for RDs in SSA, leading to underreporting through misdiagnosis, lack of diagnosis, and incorrect cause of death coding. \n\n### **_Healthcare Coverage for Rare Diseases in SSA_** \n\nAccess to healthcare is a challenge for most individuals in SSA but is especially difficult for those with RDs. Care is often unavailable, inaccessible, or unaffordable, with many affected individuals dying without a diagnosis. Stigma, discrimination, and marginalization are common, especially in rural areas where traditional health beliefs may be more common. Some affected individuals are at risk of interpersonal violence secondary to stigma, such as those living with oculocutaneous albinism. Families may hide children with RDs from their community to avoid stigma and discrimination. \n\n### **_Obstetric Screening and Prevention Efforts for Congenital Anomalies_** \n\nApproximately 52% of pregnant females in SSA achieve the recommended minimum of four antenatal care visits, which has recently been updated to eight. In South Africa, while 83% of pregnant females completed first antenatal care visits, less than a fifth of these visits were prior to 20 weeks of pregnancy, with most (72%) occurring between 3 and 6 months of pregnancy. \n\nAccess to skilled ultrasonography during pregnancy is limited in most of SSA. In South Africa, targeted screening, including ultrasound, is recommended only for women with high-risk pregnancies. \n\nFolate fortification for the prevention of neural tube defects is not universally mandated across SSA; South Africa has implemented mandatory folate fortification of staple crops since 2003, resulting in a 30% reduction in preventable neural tube defects. \n\n### **_Newborn Screening for Rare Diseases_** \n\nNBS varies across the region. Biochemical NBS for sickle cell disease is performed in many countries with higher prevalence. The Consortium on Newborn Screening in Africa (CONSA), launched in 2020, aims to standardize newborn hemoglobinopathy screening and early intervention for children with sickle cell disease in SSA. In South Africa, less than 5% of the million neonates born annually undergo biochemical NBS. Fragmented efforts have been largely focused on NBS for congenital hypothyroidism. \n\n### **_Resource Limitations_** \n\nThe shortage and maldistribution of health workers in the SSA region pose a challenge to achieving universal health coverage. Only four SSA countries (Namibia, Mauritius, Seychelles, and South Africa) currently have the required density of healthcare workers (>4.45 per 1,000) necessary to achieve universal health coverage. South Africa is the only country in the region with training centers for clinical geneticists and genetic counselors, but the ratio of such trained professionals to the population remains inadequate. \n\n### **_Legislation and Regulation_** \n\nUniversal health coverage is absent or in the very early stages of implementation in most SSA countries; many have dual or multifaceted health systems, with limited state-funded services and private services. The relevant national RD legislation in most countries is broad, limited mainly to national health acts and other laws related to medicines and medical products with limited local implementation. South Africa has a comprehensive legislative and regulatory framework; the provision of genetic services is specified in its National Health Act of 2004, and policy guidelines have been published by the South Africa National Department of Health. However, effective implementation of these policies is lacking. An RD strategic plan for South Africa is being developed by the Rare Disease Access Initiative.\n\n<!-- p.72 -->\n\n### **_Role of Patient Organizations_** \n\nMany SSA countries have established national alliances for RDs. These national bodies collaborate through regional and global alliances such as the African Alliance for Rare Diseases and Rare Diseases International. Rare disease advocacy activities underway in the region include international conferences, annual participation in Rare Disease Day, and participation in research projects such as the Sub-Saharan Congenital Anomalies Network (https://sscan.tghn.org/) and Ubomi Buhle project (https://ubomibuhle.org.za/), which is dedicated to improving pregnancy outcomes and minimizing preventable congenital anomalies. \n\n### **Southeast Asia** \n\nMore than 25% of the world’s population lives in Southeast Asia. The WHO Southeast Asia Region is composed of 11 member states: Bangladesh, Bhutan, the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea, India, Indonesia, Maldives, Myanmar, Nepal, Sri Lanka, Thailand, and Timor-Leste. The region is exceptionally diverse in terms of economy, culture, ethnic groups, and health system structure. \n\n### **_Demographics and Epidemiology_** \n\nThe population in the region has been growing slowly, with the exception of India, whose population has rapidly expanded over the past decade. Most countries in the region are classified as lower middle income (i.e., Bangladesh, Bhutan, India, Myanmar, Nepal, Sri Lanka, Timor-Leste), and some are upper middle income (i.e., Indonesia, Maldives, Thailand). Southeast Asia has seen a significant reduction in maternal and child mortality, an improvement in the proportion of births attended by skilled health personnel, and a reduction in the incidence of communicable diseases over the past decade. Limited access to health services and health professionals remains a concern, however, as does readiness for national and global health risks, particularly among low-and middle-income countries. \n\n### **_Rare Disease Landscape in Southeast Asia_** \n\nUniversal healthcare coverage across Southeast Asia is mostly focused on primary preventive care and acute care; infrastructure for specialized care, particularly for RDs, is less well developed. Varying definitions of rarity have underestimated the burden of RDs in Southeast Asia, leading to inadequate resource allocation at a policy level. Diagnosis is challenging, as many specialized testing assays in lowerand middle-income countries are unavailable or must be sent abroad for completion. High costs of RD-focused management, combined with a limited number of clinical experts and facilities for most rare disorders, pose additional challenges. \n\nRD activities and initiatives vary vastly in Southeast Asia, with India, Indonesia, and Thailand having developed coordinated strategic initiatives. In India, a National Policy for Rare Diseases was formulated in 2021, which formalizes the definition and list of rare diseases and proffers an integrated \n\nand comprehensive strategy for care among PLWRD in the country. In Thailand, a governmental rare disease policy was formulated in 2020 to ensure the inclusion of 24 inborn metabolic disorders in its universal coverage package. In addition, the policy has also provided for the inclusion of 40 rare diseases in its expanded newborn screening program starting in 2022. Patient support and advocacy groups include the Organization for Rare Diseases India, Yayasan MPS dan Penyakit Langka (the Mucopolysaccharidosis and Rare Diseases Foundation) Indonesia, and the Thai Rare Disease Foundation. \n\nFuture opportunities include expanding universal healthcare coverage and ensuring rare disease care as an essential component. Enhancing diagnostic capacity, including expanding newborn screening, and increasing the number of genetic specialists and specialty care facilities will further improve diagnostic and management access. \n\n### **Western Pacific Region** \n\n#### **_Demographics and Epidemiology_** \n\nThe WHO Western Pacific Region includes almost 1.9 billion people across 37 jurisdictions and extends from Mongolia in central Asia, east to the Pitcairn Islands in the Pacific Ocean, and south to New Zealand. The region includes nations with large populations, such as China (1.4 billion people), as well as small populations, such as Niue (1,000 people). Economies vary from high to middle to low income. Some populations are rapidly expanding and transitioning from rural to urban, while others are rapidly aging. Health systems vary widely in capacity and resources, and coordinated, multisystemic, and multiagency RD initiatives vary from nonexistent or emerging to robust, with some initiatives having decades of established policy initiatives. \n\n### **_The Rare Disease Landscape in the Western Pacific Region_** \n\nIn Japan, rare diseases are referred to as Nan-Byo, and the Nan-Byo Act was established in 1972 to deliver an impartial and stable financial support system for PLWRD. Through the act, the Ministry for Health, Labour, and Welfare has allocated resources to promote research and development for RDs, and to improve hospitals, care facilities, and home care service abilities. Further sustainability of Nan-Byo interventions was supported through comprehensive tax and social security policy reform in 2013. In 2014, the Japan Agency for Medical Research and Development established the Initiative for Rare and Undiagnosed Diseases (IRUD), designed to share Japan’s RD expertise among researchers, clinicians, and patients, both nationally and internationally. The IRUD provides a centralized resource for PLWRD through a nationwide network of clinical centers, medical specialist committees, genomic analysis centers, and a central data management center for clinical and genomic records. The knowledge management platform for IRUD (i.e., IRUD Exchange) was adapted from the multinational Patient Archive platform,\n\n<!-- p.73 -->\n\nfirst developed as a partnership among the Undiagnosed Diseases Program, Western Australian Health Department, and the Kinghorn Centre for Clinical Genomics at the Garvan Institute of Medical Research in New South Wales. The initiative was extended subsequently (Beyond IRUD) to include a national approach to multi-omics and functional analyses. \n\nIn the Republic of Korea, the Rare Disease Management Act of 2015 provided national guidelines for implementing comprehensive policies for defining and managing RDs. The act additionally aims to improve access to orphan drugs and reduce the financial burden of RD care. Patient-led organizations include the Korean Organization for Rare Diseases. \n\nThe Philippines established a central laboratory in 1997 for a nationwide newborn screening system. The Rare Disease Act of the Philippines, approved in 2016, has provisions for RDs, including orphan drugs, patient care, registry formation, research and development, information sharing, and screening. The act also categorizes PLWRD as “persons with disability,” allowing for statutory benefits such as discounted healthcare services and medicines. \n\nIn 2018, the Chinese government developed a coordinated suite of national RD initiatives covering diagnosis and treatment, access to medication, affordability of care, informatics innovation, medical education, and resource allocation. The National Health Commission implemented measures including forming its first catalog of rare diseases, establishing a rare disease alliance, developing guidelines and a network for collaboration in rare disease diagnosis and treatment, creating mechanisms for sharing diagnostic and treatment information, and creating expert committees to ensure standardization of RD diagnosis and treatment and improve diagnostic and treatment capabilities nationwide. The National Medical Products Administration also drafted a series of policies to accelerate the review and approval of novel medicines. The Ministry of Finance implemented tax incentives for drugs to treat RDs, to encourage the marketing and importing of rare disease drugs, and to reduce drug costs for PLWRD. The National Healthcare Security Administration has adjusted its list of covered medicines to include an increasing number of RD drugs under basic medical insurance and has negotiated to reduce the price of some relevant drugs. The informatics innovation for diagnosis and treatment has extended to a network of more than 300 hospitals. \n\nBroad advocacy for a national action plan for RDs in Australia first came from primary care organizations in 2006; from there, advocacy built and culminated in a 2011 national multistakeholder symposium for RDs in Western Australia (WA). Out of this symposium, Rare Voices Australia (RVA) was created and went on to catalyze many national governmental and nongovernmental initiatives. The WA Health System was the first southern hemisphere jurisdiction to launch a RD plan, the WA Rare Diseases Strategic Framework, 2015–2018. The Australian government commissioned RVA to deliver the Australian National \n\nStrategic Action Plan for Rare Diseases (2020), which was followed by a series of government-funded implementation projects. The first Australian undiagnosed diseases program (UPD) in a public health system was implemented in WA Health (UPD-WA) in 2015 as part of a range of rare disease initiatives delivered through WA Health. Its development was commensurate with the co-founding of Undiagnosed Diseases Network International. Subsequently, researchbased UDPs were formed in other jurisdictions, and in 2022 a national UDP research network (UDN-Aus) commenced. \n\nIn 2023, prompted in part by a review of pharmaceutical funding, New Zealand announced its intention for a National Rare Diseases Plan. Both Australia and New Zealand identify priority populations within their plans, including Indigenous people, culturally and linguistically diverse populations, and those in remote and regional areas. The Australia Plan also identified the potential to build on existing infrastructures to support RD data collection, such as creating congenital anomaly registries. \n\nNotable regional initiatives or global initiatives with a significant regional footprint include the Undiagnosed Diseases Network International (e.g., Australia, Japan, China, Singapore, Hing Kong, New Zealand, South Korea), the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation Action Plan on Rare Diseases, the Global Nursing Network for Rare Diseases, the Asia-Pacific Alliance of Rare Diseases Organizations and the BRIDGE-ASEAN (Bringing Research Innovations for the Diagnosis of Genetic diseases in ASEAN) initiative, as well as a range of genomics-focused projects and alliances. \n"});
